hadiths[521]{hadithnumber,text}:
"472","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim.""**"
"473","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim.""**"
"474","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim.""**"
"475","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seeking knowledge is an obligation upon every Muslim.""**"
"476","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to gain deep understanding in the religion.""**"
"477","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A little knowledge is better than abundant worship. It suffices for a person's religious understanding to worship Allah, and it suffices for a person's ignorance to be conceited with his own opinion. People are only of two kinds: a believer and an ignorant person; so do not harm the believer, and do not keep company with the ignorant.""**"
"478","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""The virtue of knowledge is better than the virtue of worship, and the best of your religion is scrupulous piety (al-waraʿ).""**"
"479","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The most virtuous worship is religious understanding (fiqh), and the most virtuous aspect of religion is scrupulous piety (al-waraʿ).""**"
"480","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The virtue of knowledge is superior to worship, and the cornerstone of religion is scrupulous piety (al-waraʿ).""**"
"481","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A little understanding of religion is better than abundant worship, and the best of your deeds are the easiest of them.""**"
"482","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No two things have been joined together better than knowledge joined with forbearance (ḥilm).""**"
"483","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No acquirer has acquired anything like the virtue of knowledge that guides its possessor to right guidance or turns him back from destruction; and a person's religion is not upright until his deeds are upright.""**"
"484","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to gain understanding in the religion.""**"
"485","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whomever Allah wills good for, He grants him understanding in the religion.""**"
"486","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whomever Allah wills good for, He grants him understanding in the religion.""**"
"487","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Allah is not worshipped with anything superior to understanding in religion. A single jurist is harder upon Satan than a thousand worshippers. Everything has a pillar, and the pillar of this religion is understanding (fiqh).""**"
"488","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When Allah wills good for a servant, He grants him understanding in the religion and inspires him with his right guidance.""**"
"489","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the likeness of the scholars upon the earth is that of the stars in the heaven, by which guidance is navigated in the darkness of the land and the sea. When the stars are obscured, the guided travelers are nearly led astray.""**"
"490","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""People are like ores of metal: the best of them in the pre-Islamic period (Jāhiliyyah) are the best of them in Islam, provided they comprehend the religion.""**"
"491","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""People are of two types: a scholar and a student, and they are equal in reward; there is no good in any of the people besides them.""**"
"492","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The world is accursed, and accursed is all that is in it, except for a scholar, the remembrance of Allah, and whatever aids it.""**"
"493","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The scholar and the student are partners in goodness, and there is no good in the rest of the people.""**"
"494","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Be a scholar or a student, and do not be anything in between. If you cannot do that, then love the scholars and do not harbor hatred toward them.""**"
"495","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Be a scholar, or a student, or an attentive listener, or a lover of knowledge; do not be the fifth, lest you perish.""** ʿAṭāʾ said that Misʿar said to him: ""You added a fifth category that we did not have before."" He said: > **""And the fifth is that you hate knowledge and its people.""**"
"496","Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh reported: I went early in the morning to Ṣafwān ibn ʿAssāl al-Murādī رضي الله عنه, and he asked, ""What brings you out so early, O Zirr?"" I replied, ""I seek knowledge."" He said: > **""Be a scholar or a student, and do not be anything in between.""**"
"497","Abū al-Rudayn رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No group of people gathers over the Book of Allah, teaching and studying it among themselves, except that they are guests of Allah, and the angels surround them until they disperse or engage in other discourse. And no one departs seeking knowledge out of fear that it might perish, or transcribing it out of fear that it might be effaced, except that he is like one marching out morning and evening in the cause of Allah. And whoever is slowed down by his deeds will not be accelerated by his lineage.""**"
"498","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The scholar is favored over the worshipper by seventy degrees, the distance between each two degrees being like the distance between heaven and earth.""**"
"499","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever goes out in the morning to the mosque, desiring nothing other than to learn good or to teach it, receives a reward like that of a pilgrim whose Ḥajj is complete.""**"
"500","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever enters this mosque of mine to learn good or to teach it is at the station of a mujāhid in the cause of Allah. And whoever enters it for anything other than that, engaging in ordinary talk of the people, is in the position of one who sees something that impresses him, yet it belongs to someone else.""**"
"501","Ṣafwān ibn ʿAssāl al-Murādī رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever goes out from his home in pursuit of knowledge, the angels surely lower their wings for the student and the scholar.""**"
"502","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever seeks knowledge and attains it, Allah records for him two portions of reward; and whoever seeks knowledge but does not attain it, Allah records for him one portion of reward.""**"
"503","Sakhbarah رضي الله عنه reported: Two men passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was seated admonishing the people. He said: > **""Sit down, for you are upon goodness.""** When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up and his Companions dispersed from him, the two men stood up and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you told us: 'Sit down, for you are upon goodness.' Was that specifically for us or for all people generally?"" He ﷺ said: > **""No servant seeks knowledge except that it serves as an expiation for what has preceded.""**"
"504","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whomever his appointed time of death reaches while he is seeking knowledge will meet Allah with nothing between him and the Prophets except the single rank of prophethood.""**"
"505","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه passed by the market of Madīnah, stopped by it, and called out: ""O people of the market, what renders you so helpless?"" They asked, ""And what is that, O Abū Hurayrah?"" He said, ""There is the inheritance of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ being distributed while you remain here! Will you not go and take your share of it?"" They asked, ""Where is it?"" He replied, ""In the mosque."" So they rushed out hurriedly, and Abū Hurayrah waited for them until they returned. He asked them, ""What happened to you?"" They said, ""O Abū Hurayrah, we went to the mosque and entered, but we did not see anything being distributed therein!"" Abū Hurayrah asked them, ""Did you not see anyone in the mosque?"" They replied, ""Yes, indeed; we saw people praying, people reciting the Qur'an, and people studying the lawful and the prohibited."" Abū Hurayrah said to them: > **""Woe unto you! That is the true inheritance of Muḥammad ﷺ.""**"
"506","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whichever youth grows up immersed in knowledge and worship until he attains old age, Allah will grant him on the Day of Resurrection the reward of ninety-two Ṣiddīqs (truthful champions of faith).""**"
"507","Abū Hurayrah and Abū Dharr رضي الله عنهما said: > **""A single chapter of knowledge that a person learns is more beloved to me than offering a thousand voluntary rakʿahs of prayer.""**"
"508","Abū Hurayrah and Abū Dharr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When death arrives for a seeker of knowledge while he is in this state, he dies as a martyr.""**"
"509","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, for the seeker of knowledge, the angels spread out their wings and seek forgiveness for him.""**"
"510","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""For the teacher of good, everything seeks forgiveness, even the fish in the sea.""**"
"511","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The scholars of this Ummah are of two types of men: a man whom Allah granted knowledge, so he imparted it generously to the people, harboring no greed over it and seeking no worldly price for it—for such a person, the fish of the sea, the beasts of the land, and the birds in the expanse of the sky pray for forgiveness, and he will arrive before Allah as a noble master until he accompanies the Messengers. And a man whom Allah granted knowledge, yet he was stingy with it toward the servants of Allah, coveted material gain for it, and purchased with it a worldly price—he shall be bridled on the Day of Resurrection with a bridle of fire, and a caller will proclaim: 'This is the one whom Allah gave knowledge, yet he withheld it from the servants of Allah, took greedily for it, and bought with it a worldly price!' This proclamation will continue until the Reckoning is concluded.""**"
"512","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""For the teacher of good, everything seeks forgiveness, even the fish in the seas.""**"
"513","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah and His angels, even the ant in its burrow and the fish in the sea, send blessings upon the one who teaches people goodness.""**"
"514","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Good is abundant, but those who act upon it are few.""**"
"515","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of one who learns knowledge in his youth is like an engraving upon stone, and the likeness of one who learns knowledge in old age is like one who writes upon water.""**"
"516","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whichever youth grows up immersed in knowledge and worship until he reaches old age, Allah will grant him on the Day of Resurrection the reward of ninety-two Ṣiddīqs.""**"
"517","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Allah never sent any prophet except that he was a young man, nor was any scholar granted knowledge except while he was a young man.""**"
"518","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Luqmān said to his son: 'O my dear son, adhere to sitting in the company of the scholars, and listen to the speech of the wise; for indeed, Allah revives the dead heart with the light of wisdom just as He revives the barren earth with torrential rain.'""**"
"519","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Sit in the company of the elders, question the scholars, and mingle with the wise.""**"
"520","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""The God-fearing are masters, the jurists are leaders, and sitting in their company is an increase (in goodness and knowledge).""**"
"521","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you pass by the gardens of Paradise, graze therein.""** They asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what are the gardens of Paradise?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""The assemblies of knowledge.""**"
"522","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, have mercy upon my successors!""** We said, ""O Messenger of Allah, who are your successors?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Those who come after me, who narrate my traditions (hadiths) and teach them to the people.""**"
"523","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The scholars are the successors of the Prophets.""**"
"524","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I surely know of people who are neither prophets nor martyrs, yet the prophets and martyrs will envy them for their exalted rank on the Day of Resurrection: those who love Allah and make Him beloved to His creation, commanding them with obedience to Allah; so when they obey Allah, Allah loves them.""**"
"525","A man from Banū ʿAbs reported: I was walking alongside Sulaymān along the bank of the Tigris river when he said, ""O brother of Banū ʿAbs, descend and drink."" So I drank. Then he said, ""Drink again."" So I drank again. He then asked, ""How much has your drinking diminished from the Tigris?"" I replied, ""It has diminished nothing!"" He said: > **""Indeed, knowledge is likewise: it is taken from, yet it does not diminish.""**"
"526","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""O people, you must adhere to knowledge before it is taken away, and its taking away is the departure of its people. You must adhere to knowledge, for none of you knows when he will be in dire need of what he possesses. You must adhere to knowledge, and beware of pedantry and excessive hair-splitting. And adhere to the ancient, original matter (al-ʿatīq), for there will surely come a people who recite the Book of Allah yet cast it behind their backs.""**"
"527","Thaʿlabah ibn al-Ḥakam رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say to the scholars on the Day of Resurrection, when He is seated upon His Throne to judge among His servants: 'I did not place My knowledge and My forbearance within you except that I intended to forgive you for whatever was in you, and I do not mind.'""**"
"528","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah will resurrect the servants on the Day of Resurrection, then He will distinguish the scholars and say: 'O assembly of scholars, I did not place My knowledge within you in order to punish you. Go forth, for I have indeed forgiven you.'""**"
"529","Ḥizām ibn Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām narrated from his father رضي الله عنه: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, you have entered an era whose jurists are many and whose orators are few, whose givers are many and whose beggars are few; in it, deeds are better than mere knowledge. But there will come an era whose jurists will be few and whose orators will be many, whose askers will be many and whose givers will be few; in it, knowledge will be better than mere deeds.""**"
"530","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, you are in an era whose scholars are many and whose orators are few; whoever among you leaves even a fraction of one-tenth of what he knows will fall into perdition. But there will come upon the people an era whose scholars will be few and whose orators will be many; whoever holds fast in it to even one-tenth of what he knows will be saved.""**"
"531","Ḥizām ibn Ḥakīm narrated from his paternal uncle رضي الله عنه: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You have entered an era whose jurists are many and whose orators are few, whose givers are many and whose seekers are few; in it, practice is better than knowledge. And there shall come an era whose jurists will be few and whose orators will be many, whose seekers will be many and whose givers will be few; in it, knowledge will be better than practice.""**"
"532","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""He is not of my Ummah who does not venerate our elders, show compassion to our young ones, and recognize the rightful due of our scholar.""**"
"533","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There are three whom none treats with contempt except a hypocrite: a person of grey hair in Islam, a possessor of sacred knowledge, and a just leader.""**"
"534","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""I do not fear for my Ummah except three traits: that worldly wealth will become abundant among them and they will envy one another and fight; that the Book will be opened to them and a believer will take it seeking its hidden interpretation, whereas none knows its interpretation except Allah, while those firmly rooted in knowledge say: 'We believe in it, all is from our Lord,' and none remember except people of understanding; and that they will see their scholar and neglect him, paying him no regard.""**"
"535","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever teaches a servant an āyah from the Book of Allah is his patron; it is not befitting for him to abandon him nor to act selfishly against him.""**"
"536","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of one who hears words of wisdom but then narrates the worst of what he heard is like a man who came to a shepherd and said, 'O shepherd, grant me a sheep from your flock to slaughter.' The shepherd said, 'Go and seize the ear of the best sheep among them.' So the man went and seized the ear of the flock's watchdog.""**"
"537","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""O people! Knowledge is only attained through learning, and deep understanding is only attained through seeking comprehension. Whomever Allah wills good for, He grants him understanding in the religion; and indeed, 'only those among His servants who possess knowledge fear Allah.'""**"
"538","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Knowledge is only acquired through learning, and forbearance is only attained through cultivating forbearance. Whoever strives earnestly for good will be granted it, and whoever strives to guard against evil will be protected from it. There are three traits: whoever possesses them will not dwell in the exalted ranks—and I do not tell you he will not enter Paradise: one who practices soothsaying, one who seeks omens through divining arrows, or one who is turned back from his journey by superstition.""**"
"539","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""You must adhere to this Qur'an, for indeed it is the banquet of Allah. Whoever among you is able to partake of the banquet of Allah, let him do so; for knowledge is only acquired through learning.""**"
"540","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, gatherings are of three kinds: safe from sin, gaining spiritual spoil, or ruined in loss.""**"
"541","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""People are of three types: one who emerges safe, one who attains spiritual gain, and one who incurs perdition.""**"
"542","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Teach, make matters easy, and do not make things difficult.""**"
"543","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: A young man from Quraysh came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me permission to commit fornication."" The people turned upon him and rebuked him sharply, saying, ""Stop! Stop!"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Bring him close.""** So he drew near to him, and the Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""Would you like it for your mother?""** He replied, ""No, by Allah, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Neither do the people like it for their mothers.""** He ﷺ asked: > **""Would you like it for your daughter?""** He replied, ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Neither do the people like it for their daughters.""** He ﷺ asked: > **""Would you like it for your sister?""** He replied, ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Neither do the people like it for their sisters.""** He ﷺ asked: > **""Would you like it for your paternal aunt?""** He replied, ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Neither do the people like it for their paternal aunts.""** He ﷺ asked: > **""Would you like it for your maternal aunt?""** He replied, ""No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Neither do the people like it for their maternal aunts.""** The Prophet ﷺ then placed his hand upon him and supplicated: > **""O Allah, forgive his sin, purify his heart, and guard his chastity.""** The narrator stated: After that, the young man never paid heed to anything illicit again."
"544","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: > **""Whenever the Prophet ﷺ spoke, he would repeat his speech three times so that he might be thoroughly understood.""**"
"545","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Acquire knowledge, and acquire along with knowledge serenity and dignity, and humble yourselves before those from whom you learn.""**"
"546","Jamīlah, the mother of the child (*umm walad*) of Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, narrated: Whenever Thābit [al-Bunānī] came to Anas, Anas would say: > **""O young maidservant! Bring me perfume so that I may wipe my hands with it, for indeed, the son of the mother of Thābit will not be satisfied until he kisses my hand.""**"
"547","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My brother Mūsā عليه السلام supplicated: 'O my Lord, show me the person whom You showed me upon the ship.' So Allah revealed to him: 'O Mūsā, you shall soon see him.' Before long, al-Khiḍr came to him, exuding a pleasant fragrance and dressed in fine white garments. He said: 'Peace be upon you, O Mūsā ibn ʿImrān! Indeed, your Lord conveys to you peace and the mercy of Allah.' Mūsā replied: 'He is Peace (al-Salām), from Him is peace, and unto Him all peace returns. All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, whose blessings I cannot enumerate, and whose gratitude I am incapable of fulfilling except through His aid.' Then Mūsā said: 'Indeed, I desire that you impart to me an admonition through which Allah may grant me benefit after you.'** > > **Al-Khiḍr said: 'O seeker of knowledge! Indeed, the speaker is less susceptible to boredom than the listener; therefore, do not weary your companions when you speak to them. Know that your heart is a vessel, so observe carefully what you fill your vessel with. Turn away from the world and cast it behind your back, for it is neither an enduring home for you nor a place of permanence; rather, it was made merely as a provision for servants to take provisions from it toward the ultimate Return. O Mūsā, accustom yourself to patience and you will attain forbearance; make your heart conscious of Taqwa and you will attain knowledge; and discipline your soul with patience to be liberated from sin. O Mūsā, devote yourself entirely to knowledge if you truly desire it, for knowledge belongs only to him who devotes himself completely to it. Do not be excessively talkative or a babbler in speech, for abundant talk disgraces scholars and exposes the deficiencies of the foolish; rather, adhere to moderation, for that is part of divine guidance and rectitude. Turn away from the ignorant and be forbearing toward the foolish, for that is the virtue of the wise and the adornment of scholars. When an ignorant person reviles you, remain silent before him in peace, and avoid him with resolve, for the evil of his ignorance against you and his insults will rebound upon him greater and more severely. O son of ʿImrān, never open a door unless you know how to close it, and never close a door unless you know how to open it. O son of ʿImrān, how can he whose greed for the world does not cease, and whose craving for it never ends, be a true worshipper? And how can one who despises his condition and accuses Allah regarding what He has decreed for him be an ascetic? Can someone whose whims have overcome him restrain himself from desires? And can seeking knowledge benefit someone enveloped in ignorance—while his journey is toward his Hereafter yet he turns his face toward his worldly life? O Mūsā, learn that which you learn so that you may act upon it; do not learn it merely to narrate it to others, lest its ruin be upon you while its light benefits someone else. O son of ʿImrān, make asceticism and Taqwa your garments, knowledge and remembrance your speech, and perform righteous deeds in abundance, for you are bound to commit errors; shake your heart with the fear of Allah, for that pleases your Lord; and do good, for you cannot avoid doing otherwise. I have admonished you if you preserve it.' Then al-Khiḍr departed, leaving Mūsā sorrowful and deeply burdened.""**"
"548","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Teach, make things easy, and do not make things difficult. And when you become angry, remain silent; when you become angry, remain silent; when you become angry, remain silent.""**"
"549","Umm al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنها narrated: Abū al-Dardāʾ never narrated a hadith without smiling while doing so. I said to him, ""I fear that people might regard you as foolish."" He said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never narrated a hadith without smiling during it.""**"
"550","Ṣafwān ibn ʿAssāl al-Murādī رضي الله عنه narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in the mosque, reclining upon a red cloak of his. I said to him, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have come seeking knowledge."" He ﷺ said: > **""Welcome to the seeker of knowledge! Indeed, the angels surround the seeker of knowledge with their wings, then they mount one upon another until they reach the lowest heaven out of their love for what he seeks.""**"
"551","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه: > **""Indeed, Allah has commanded me to teach you and not to treat you harshly, and to draw you near and not to distance you. Thus, it is my duty to teach you, and it is your duty to comprehend and preserve it.""**"
"552","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Go early in the morning in search of knowledge, for I asked my Lord to bless my Ummah in its early morning hours, and to make that on Thursdays.""**"
"553","Qurrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat down, his Companions would sit around him in circles.""**"
"554","Yazīd al-Raqāshī narrated: Whenever Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated this hadith to us, he would say: > **""By Allah, it was not as you and your companions do—where one of you sits down, they gather around him, and he delivers a speech. Rather, when they performed the morning prayer, they would sit in circles reciting the Qur'an and learning the obligatory duties and the Sunan.""**"
"555","Qabīṣah ibn al-Mukhāriq رضي الله عنه narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said to me, ""O Qabīṣah, what brings you here?"" I said, ""My age has advanced and my bones have grown frail, so I came to you that you may teach me something by which Allah will benefit me."" He ﷺ said: > **""O Qabīṣah, you did not pass by any rock, tree, or clay dwelling except that it sought forgiveness for you. O Qabīṣah, when you pray the morning prayer, say three times: 'Subḥān Allāh al-ʿAẓīm wa biḥamdihi' (Glory be to Allah, the Magnificent, and praise be to Him)—you will be protected from blindness, leprosy, and paralysis. O Qabīṣah, say: 'Allāhumma innī as'aluka mimmā ʿindaka, wa afiḍ ʿalayya min faḍlika, wanshur ʿalayya min raḥmatika, wa anzil ʿalayya min barakātika' (O Allah, I ask You of what is with You, pour over me of Your bounty, spread over me of Your mercy, and send down upon me of Your blessings).""**"
"556","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No one leaves his house except that at his door are two banners: a banner in the hand of an angel and a banner in the hand of a devil. If he goes out for a purpose that Allah loves, the angel follows him with his banner, and he remains under the banner of the angel until he returns to his home. And if he goes out for a purpose that displeases Allah, Mighty and Majestic, the devil follows him with his banner, and he remains under the banner of the devil until he returns to his home.""**"
"557","ʿAlī [ibn Abī Ṭālib] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No servant puts on his sandals, leather socks, or clothing to set out in search of knowledge except that Allah forgives him his sins the moment he steps over the threshold of his door.""**"
"558","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No man leaves his home seeking knowledge except that Allah makes easy for him thereby a path to Paradise.""**"
"559","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: We were sitting with Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه when a funeral procession passed by. He stood up, so we stood up, and then we prayed. He took off his sandals, so we said, ""O Successor of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, you took off your sandals while the people are wearing their sandals?"" He replied: > **""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever walks barefoot in obedience to Allah, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will not question him on the Day of Resurrection regarding what He made obligatory upon him.'""**"
"560","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you hasten toward good, walk barefoot, for Allah doubles the reward of such a person over one who wears sandals.""**"
"561","ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl narrated that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه say: News reached me concerning a man who had heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So I purchased a camel, strapped on my saddle, and journeyed toward him for a whole month until I arrived in the Levant, where I found ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays رضي الله عنه. I said to the doorkeeper, ""Tell him that Jābir is at the door."" He asked, ""The son of ʿAbdullāh?"" I said, ""Yes."" So he came out, treading upon his garment [in haste], and he embraced me and I embraced him. I said, ""A hadith reached me regarding you, that you heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning retribution (*qiṣāṣ*), and I feared that you might die or I might die before I could hear it."" He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Allah will gather mankind on the Day of Resurrection—or he said: the servants—naked, uncircumcised, and *buhm*.""** We asked, ""What is *buhm*?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Having nothing with them. Then He will call them with a voice that those far away will hear just as those nearby hear: 'I am the Judge (*al-Dayyān*), I am the Sovereign (*al-Mālik*). It is not proper for any of the people of the Fire to enter the Fire while he is owed a right by anyone among the people of Paradise until I settle it for him; and it is not proper for any of the people of Paradise to enter Paradise while he is owed a right by anyone among the people of the Fire until I settle it for him—even if it be a single slap.'""** We said, ""How can this be when we will come naked, uncircumcised, and destitute?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Through good deeds and bad deeds.""**"
"562","Makḥūl narrated: ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه came to Maslamah ibn Makhlad رضي الله عنه, and there was some altercation between him and the doorkeeper. Maslamah heard his voice and granted him permission to enter. ʿUqbah said, ""I have not come to visit you socially; rather, I came to you for a specific need. Do you remember the day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Whoever learns of a misdeed committed by his brother and conceals it, Allah will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection.""** Maslamah replied, ""Yes."" ʿUqbah said, ""It is for this that I came."""
"563","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr narrated from Munīb, from his uncle, who said: Word reached a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ concerning another man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who was residing in Egypt, that he was narrating from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever conceals the fault of his Muslim brother in this world, Allah will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection.""** So he traveled to him in Egypt and asked him about the hadith. The companion said, ""Yes, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever conceals the fault of his Muslim brother in this world, Allah will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection.""** The visiting companion said, ""And I, too, heard it directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."""
"564","Ibn Jurayj narrated: Abū Ayyūb [al-Anṣārī] رضي الله عنه mounted his riding beast and journeyed to ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه in Egypt. He said, ""I am asking you about a matter regarding which no one who witnessed it directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ remains except you and me. What did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say regarding concealing the faults of a Muslim?"" ʿUqbah replied: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever conceals the private fault (*ʿawrah*) of a believer in this world, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will conceal his faults on the Day of Resurrection.""** So Abū Ayyūb turned back toward Medina, and he did not even untie his saddle until he had heard this hadith."
"565","Rajāʾ ibn Ḥaywah narrated: I heard Maslamah ibn Makhlad رضي الله عنه say: While I was governing Egypt, the doorkeeper came and said, ""A bedouin is at the gate upon a camel asking permission to enter."" I asked, ""Who are you?"" He replied, ""Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī."" Maslamah said: I looked down upon him and said, ""Shall I descend to you, or will you come up?"" He replied, ""Neither shall you descend nor shall I come up. A hadith reached me that you narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning concealing the faults of a believer, and I came solely to hear it."" I said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever conceals a private fault (*ʿawrah*) of a believer, it is as though he has revived a female infant buried alive (*mawʾūdah*).""** Thereupon, Jābir struck his camel and turned back on his journey."
"566","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""People will set out from the East and the West in search of knowledge, but they will not find any scholar more knowledgeable than the scholar of Medina—or the scholar of the people of Medina.""**"
"567","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه addressed the people at al-Jābiyah, saying: > **""O people! Whoever wishes to ask about the Qur'an, let him go to Ubayy ibn Kaʿb. Whoever wishes to ask about the laws of inheritance (*farāʾiḍ*), let him go to Zayd ibn Thābit. Whoever wishes to ask about jurisprudence (*fiqh*), let him go to Muʿādh ibn Jabal. And whoever wishes to ask about wealth, let him come to me, for indeed, Allah has appointed me as its custodian and distributor.""**"
"568","Abū Umayyah al-Jumaḥī رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ about the Hour, and he ﷺ replied: > **""Among its portents are three: one of them is seeking knowledge from the *aṣāghir* (the minor ones / subordinates).""** Mūsā [the narrator] said: ""It is said that *al-aṣāghir* refers to the people of religious innovations (*ahl al-bidaʿ*)."""
"569","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""The people will continue to remain upright and cohesive so long as knowledge comes to them from the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ and from their elders; but when knowledge comes to them from their youngsters (or subordinates), they will be ruined.""**"
"570","ʿAlī [ibn Abī Ṭālib] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Hadith is to be understood according to what you commonly recognize.""**"
"571","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There are two avid seekers whose greed is never sated: the seeker of knowledge and the seeker of the worldly life.""**"
"572","Mujāhid narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما—the narrator said: I believe he raised it to the Prophet ﷺ—who said: > **""There are two insatiable seekers whose craving never ends: an insatiable seeker in the pursuit of knowledge whose craving is never extinguished, and an insatiable seeker in the pursuit of the worldly life whose craving is never extinguished.""**"
"573","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Four things are never sated with four: an eye with looking, the earth with rain, a female with a male, and a scholar with knowledge.""**"
"574","Jābir [ibn ʿAbdillāh] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Among the foundations of Taqwa is that you learn, alongside what you already know, that which you do not know; and deficiency in what you already know is the lack of increase therein. Indeed, what causes a man to become indifferent to learning what he does not know is his meager benefit from what he has already learned.""**"
"575","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If a day passes over me in which I do not increase in knowledge, then may no blessing be placed in the rising of the sun on that day.""**"
"576","ʿAṭāʾ narrated: I was with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما when a man came to him and said, ""O Abū ʿAbbās, what do you say concerning me?"" Ibn ʿAbbās said, ""And what should I say concerning you?"" The man said, ""I work with the pen."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The person of the pen will be brought on the Day of Resurrection in a casket of fire, locked with padlocks of fire, and his pen will be examined as to what he used it for. If he used it in obedience to Allah and His pleasure, the casket will be unlocked for him; but if he used it in disobedience to Allah, he will plummet with it in the casket for seventy autumns—even down to the sharpening of the pen and the ink poured into the inkstand.""**"
"577","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever invokes blessings upon me in a book, the angels continue to seek forgiveness for him for as long as my name remains in that book.""**"
"578","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever remembers me, let him send blessings upon me.""**"
"579","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever hears me mentioned in his presence, let him invoke blessings upon me.""**"
"580","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever hears me mentioned in his presence and fails to invoke blessings upon me has missed the path to Paradise.""**"
"581","Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You hear, and others will hear from you, and others will hear from those who hear from you.""** Then he ﷺ said: > **""After that, there will come a people who bear witness before being asked to bear witness.""**"
"582","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said during the Farewell Pilgrimage: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my saying and comprehends it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding is not himself a jurist. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere counsel to the leaders of the Muslims, and adhering to their main body (*jamāʿah*); for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"583","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon to us and said: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears this saying of mine and conveys it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who possesses deeper understanding than himself. There are three things concerning which the heart of a Muslim never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, sincere counsel to every Muslim, and adhering to the main body of the Muslims; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"584","ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr narrated from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet ﷺ addressed them, saying: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my saying and comprehends it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding possesses no profound understanding himself, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who possesses deeper understanding than he.""**"
"585","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a servant who hears my speech and then adds nothing to it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who retains it better than he. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere counsel to those in authority, and holding fast to the main body of the Muslims; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"586","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه narrated in a sermon: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us in Masjid al-Khayf, saying: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a servant who hears my saying and carries it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding is not himself a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who has deeper understanding than he. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere counsel to those in authority, and adhering to the main body of the Muslims; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"587","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr narrated from his father [Bashīr ibn Saʿd] رضي الله عنهما, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""May Allah have mercy upon a servant who hears my speech and memorizes it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding is not himself a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who has deeper understanding than he. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere counsel to the leaders of the Muslims, and adhering to the main body of the Muslims.""**"
"588","Abū Qirṣāfah Jandarah ibn Khaythamah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my saying, comprehends it, and memorizes it; for perhaps a carrier of knowledge conveys it to one who is more knowledgeable than he. There are three things concerning which the heart never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds, offering sincere counsel to those in authority, and adhering to the main body (*al-jamāʿah*).""** [The narrator added: Word reached me that a son of Abū Qirṣāfah was captured by the Byzantines. Abū Qirṣāfah used to call out to him from the city wall of Ascalon at the time of each prayer: ""O so-and-so! The prayer!"" and his son would hear him and answer him, even though the expanse of the sea lay between them.]"
"589","Jābir [ibn ʿAbdillāh] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my saying, comprehends it, and then conveys it; for perhaps the one to whom it is conveyed is more retentive than the listener. There are three things concerning which the heart of a Muslim person never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere counsel to the rulers of the Muslims, and adhering to their main body; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"590","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a servant who hears my saying and comprehends it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding is not himself a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who possesses deeper understanding than he.""**"
"591","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us in Masjid al-Khayf at Minā, saying: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a person who hears my saying, memorizes it, and then delivers it to one who has not heard it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding is not himself a jurist, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who possesses deeper understanding than he. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere advice to whomever Allah has placed in authority over you, and adhering to the main body of the Muslims; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"592","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: > **""Indeed, I speak these words to you, so let the one among you who is present convey them to the one who is absent.""**"
"593","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say at al-Khayf—the Khayf of Minā: > **""May Allah brighten the face of a servant who hears my saying, memorizes it, comprehends it, and conveys it to whoever has not heard it; for perhaps a carrier of understanding possesses no profound understanding himself, and perhaps a carrier of understanding conveys it to one who possesses deeper understanding than he. There are three things concerning which the heart of a believer never harbors rancor: sincerity of deeds for Allah alone, offering sincere advice to the leaders of the Muslims, and adhering to their main body; for indeed, their supplication encompasses those behind them.""**"
"594","Wābiṣah [ibn Maʿbad] رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering a sermon during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and he said: > **""Let the one who is present convey to the one who is absent.""**"
"595","Wābiṣah [ibn Maʿbad] رضي الله عنه used to stand before the people in al-Raqqah at the Grand Mosque on the Day of Fiṭr and the Day of Sacrifice (*al-Naḥr*), saying: I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage while he was addressing the people. He said: > **""O people, which month is the most sacred?""** They said, ""This month."" He ﷺ said: > **""O people, which land is the most sacred?""** They said, ""This land."" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, your blood, your wealth, and your honor are inviolable among you, just as the sacredness of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this city of yours, until the Day you meet your Lord. Have I conveyed the message?""** The people said, ""Yes!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands toward the sky and said: > **""O Allah, bear witness!""** Then he ﷺ said: > **""O people, let the one who is present among you convey to the one who is absent.""** [Wābiṣah then said to the people:] ""Come close, so that we may convey to you just as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed us."""
"596","Makḥūl narrated: Ibn Abī Zakariyyā, Sulaymān ibn Ḥabīb, and I entered upon Abū Umāmah [al-Bāhilī] رضي الله عنه in Ḥimṣ. We greeted him with salām, and he said: > **""Indeed, this gathering of yours is part of Allah's conveyance to you and His proof against you. And indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has conveyed the message, so convey it you also.""**"
"597","Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir narrated: We used to sit with Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, and he would narrate many hadiths to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whenever he finished speaking, he would say: > **""Have you understood? Then convey as it was conveyed to you.""**"
"598","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""In the early era of this Ummah, the young learn from their elders; but in its later era, the elders will learn from their youngsters.""** It was said to Ibn ʿAbbās, ""Why is that?"" He replied: > **""Because the youngsters will have heard and learned, while the elders did not.""**"
"599","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه, when death approached him, said: > **""O my sons! Indeed, I forbid you from three things, so preserve them: do not accept a hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except from a trustworthy person (*thiqah*); do not incur debts even if you have to wear coarse wool (*ʿabāʾah*); and do not write poetry so as to occupy your hearts away from the Qur'an.""**"
"600","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Soon there will appear among you devils whom Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd [عليهما السلام] imprisoned in the sea. They will pray with you in your mosques, recite the Qur'an with you, and dispute with you concerning religion; yet indeed, they are devils in human form.""**"
"601","Abū Hurayrah and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهم reported as a raised (*marfūʿ*) narration that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""This knowledge shall be carried by the trustworthy (*ʿudūl*) of every successive generation; they will purge from it the distortions of extremists, the false interpretations of the ignorant, and the plagiarisms of the falsehood-mongers.""**"
"602","Al-Muqannaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated: I brought the zakat of our camels to the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered that it be accepted. I said, ""Among them are two she-camels as a gift for you."" So he ordered that the gift be set apart from the zakat. I remained for some days, and the people began spreading rumors that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sending Khālid ibn al-Walīd to the slaves of Miṣr—or he said: Muḍar (the narrator Abū Ghassān was unsure)—to collect their zakat. I said, ""By Allah, whatever property we and our people possess, I have already paid its zakat here."" So I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was upon a she-camel of his, accompanied by a tall Black man whose head was level with the head of the Prophet ﷺ—I had never seen anyone among the people taller than him. When I drew close, the man leaned toward me [to restrain me], but the Prophet ﷺ stopped him. I said, ""The people are spreading rumors saying such-and-such."" Thereupon, the Prophet ﷺ raised his hands until I could see the whiteness of his armpits, and he said: > **""O Allah, I do not make it lawful for them to attribute lies to me!""** Al-Muqannaʿ said: ""Therefore, I never narrate anything from the Prophet ﷺ except a hadith explicitly spoken by the Book of Allah or established by the Sunnah. If lies were attributed to him during his lifetime, how much more so after his death?"""
"603","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""The destruction of my Ummah lies in sectarian tribalism (*ʿaṣabiyyah*), denial of the Divine Decree (*qadariyyah*), and narrating traditions from unverified sources.""**"
"604","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The destruction of my Ummah lies in three things: denial of the Divine Decree (*al-qadariyyah*), tribal fanaticism (*al-ʿaṣabiyyah*), and narrating traditions from unverified sources.""**"
"605","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Offer sincere advice to one another in knowledge, for indeed, the betrayal of one of you in his knowledge is more severe than his betrayal in his wealth. And indeed, Allah will question you on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"606","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه said: > **""I heard several hadiths from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which I comprehended and memorized; nothing prevents me from narrating them except that my companions differ with me concerning them.""**"
"607","Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī narrated: I saw Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه, whenever he finished narrating a hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, say: > **""This, or something similar to it, or resembling it.""**"
"608","Muṭarrif narrated: ʿImrān ibn al-Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه said to me: ""O Muṭarrif, by Allah, I consider that if I wished, I could narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for two consecutive days without repeating a single hadith. Yet what increases my hesitation and reluctance is that men among the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ—I was present just as they were present, and I heard just as they heard—narrate hadiths that have become confused to them."" At times, ʿImrān would say: ""If I were to tell you that I heard the Prophet of Allah ﷺ say such-and-such, I believe I would be speaking the truth,"" and at other times, he would speak with firm resolve, saying: > **""I heard the Prophet of Allah ﷺ say such-and-such.""**"
"609","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the deed of Paradise?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Truthfulness. For when a servant speaks the truth, he acts righteously; when he acts righteously, he has faith; and when he has faith, he enters Paradise.""** The man asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the deed of the Fire?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Lying. When a servant lies, he commits wickedness; when he commits wickedness, he disbelieves; and when he disbelieves, he enters—meaning the Fire.""**"
"610","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""There was no character trait more detestable to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than lying. Whenever he noticed anything of that in any person, it would not leave his heart until he knew that the person had initiated sincere repentance.""** In another narration: > **""There was no character trait more detestable to the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ [than lying].""**"
"611","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها narrated: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if one of us women says regarding something she desires, 'I do not desire it,' is that counted as a lie?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, a lie is recorded as a lie, until even a small, trivial lie (*al-kudhaybah*) is recorded as a small lie.""**"
"612","Al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is a grave betrayal that you speak to your brother a statement which he believes you to be truthful in, while you are lying to him.""**"
"613","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever says to a child, 'Come here, take this,' and then does not give him anything, it is counted as a lie.""**"
"614","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""O people! What drives you to plunge headlong into lying just as moths plunge continuously into fire?""**"
"615","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever intentionally attributes a lie to me, or rejects anything that I have commanded, let him take his abode in Hellfire.""**"
"616","Dujayn Abū al-Ghuṣn narrated: I entered Medina and met Aslam, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه. I said, ""Narrate to me from ʿUmar."" He replied, ""I cannot, for I fear that I may add or omit something. Whenever we said to ʿUmar, 'Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,' he would say: 'I fear that I might add a letter or omit a letter, for indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:'"" > **""Whoever lies against me is in the Fire.""** [And in another narration: ""Whoever intentionally lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""]"
"617","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه used to say: ""Nothing prevents me from narrating from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that I was not among the most retentive of his Companions regarding what he said; rather, I bear witness that I heard him say:"" > **""Whoever attributes to me that which I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** And in another narration from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever attributes a lie to me, let him take a house in the Fire.""** And in the narration of al-Bazzār: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever intentionally lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"618","ʿAlī [ibn Abī Ṭālib] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever intentionally lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** [Al-Haythamī noted: In the Ṣaḥīḥ, from ʿAlī, the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Do not lie against me, for whoever lies against me shall enter the Fire.""]"
"619","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Whoever intentionally lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"620","Saʿīd ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, attributing a lie to me is not like attributing a lie to anyone else. Whoever intentionally lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"621","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the one who lies against me, a house will be built for him in the Fire.""**"
"622","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, Allah will build for him a house in the Fire.""**"
"623","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"624","Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah رضي الله عنه said concerning al-Mukhtār, ""This man is a liar, and I indeed heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"625","Yaḥyā ibn Maymūn al-Ḥaḍramī reported that Abū Mūsā al-Ghāfiqī heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه narrating hadiths from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon the pulpit. Abū Mūsā said, ""Indeed, this companion of yours is either a true preserver or ruined! For the last covenant the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made with us was that he said:"" > **""Hold fast to the Book of Allah. You will soon return to a people who love to hear hadiths from me; so whoever attributes to me what I have not said, let him take his seat in the Fire, and whoever has memorized something accurately, let him narrate it.""**"
"626","Hishām ibn Abī Ruqayyah reported: I heard Maslamah ibn Makhlad delivering a sermon to the people from the pulpit, saying, ""O people! Do you not find in striped cloth and linen sufficient means to dispense with silk? Here is a man among you who will inform you from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Stand up, O ʿUqbah!"" ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه stood up and said, ""Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** He then added, ""And I bear witness that I heard him say:"" > **""Whoever wears silk in this world shall be forbidden from wearing it in the Hereafter.""**"
"627","Abū Ḥayyān al-Taymī narrated from his uncle, who said: Ḥuṣayn ibn Sabrah, ʿUmar ibn Muslim, and I went to Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه. When we sat with him, Ḥuṣayn said, ""You have attained immense good, O Zayd!"" Zayd told them that ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ziyād had summoned him and asked, ""What are these hadiths you narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that we do not find in the Book of Allah? You claim he has a Basin (al-Ḥawḍ) in Paradise!"" Zayd replied, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated it to us and promised it to us."" Ibn Ziyād retorted, ""You have lied; rather, you are an old man who has grown senile!"" Zayd said, ""Indeed, my two ears heard it and my heart retained it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** Zayd then said, ""And I have never lied against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."""
"628","Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take a resting place in the Fire, or a house in Hell.""**"
"629","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me in order to mislead people thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"630","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Among the gravest of fabrications is for a person to claim to have seen with his eyes what they did not see, and among the gravest of fabrications is for someone to attribute to me what I have not said.""**"
"631","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever lies against me in narrating a hadith, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"632","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"633","ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah reported: My father and I went to an in-law of ours from the tribe of Aslam who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. I heard him say, ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Relieve us with it, O Bilāl'—referring to the prayer."" I asked, ""Did you hear this directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" He became angry and recounted to them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once dispatched a man to an Arab tribe. When he reached them, he claimed, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded me to rule among your women as I see fit!"" They said, ""We hear and obey the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" However, they sent an envoy to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who informed him, ""So-and-so came to us and claimed that the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to rule among our women; if this was indeed your command, we hear and obey, but if it was otherwise, we wished to inform you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry and dispatched a man from the Anṣār, saying: > **""Go to him, kill him, and burn him with fire.""** The Anṣāri found that the man had already died and been buried, so he ordered his grave to be exhumed and burned his corpse with fire. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** The Companion then said to me, ""Do you think that after witnessing this, I would ever lie against the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"""
"634","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: A man wore a garment resembling the robe of the Prophet ﷺ, went to a household in Madinah, and claimed, ""The Prophet ﷺ commanded me to inspect whichever household I wish!"" They said, ""Our knowledge of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is that he never commands lewdness."" They accommodated him in a house and sent a messenger to inform the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما: > **""Go to him; if you find him alive, kill him and burn him with fire, and if you find him dead, you have been spared him—and I reckon you have already been spared him—so burn him.""** They went and found that he had gone out at night to urinate, was bitten by a viper, and died; so they burned his body with fire. They returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"635","Zayd ibn Arqam and al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"636","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"637","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"638","ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"639","Nubayṭ ibn Sharīṭ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"640","Abū Maryam reported: I heard ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه saying to Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه, ""I adjure you by Allah, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire?""** Abū Mūsā remained silent and did not say anything."
"641","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me in order to mislead people thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"642","ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me in order to mislead people thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"643","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"644","ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"645","Al-ʿUrs ibn ʿAmīrah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"646","Yaʿlā ibn Murrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"647","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"648","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take a house in the Fire.""**"
"649","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take a house in the Fire. And whoever rejects a hadith that reaches him from me, I will be his adversary on the Day of Resurrection. So when a hadith reaches you from me that you do not recognize, say: 'Allah knows best.'""**"
"650","ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, the agent of al-Zubayr ibn Shuʿayb al-Baṣrī, reported that the sons of Ṣuhayb said to Ṣuhayb رضي الله عنه, ""O our father! The sons of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrate from their fathers, (so why do you not narrate to us)?"" He replied: ""I heard the Prophet ﷺ say:"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"651","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"652","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever deliberately narrates a false hadith from me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"653","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat between the two eyes of Hell.""** This weighed heavily upon his Companions, and they said, ""O Messenger of Allah! When we narrate hadiths, we may inadvertently add or omit something!"" He ﷺ replied: > **""I do not mean you; I only mean the one who lies against me in narration seeking thereby to bring disgrace upon Islam.""** They asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, you said 'between the two eyes of Hell'—does Hell have two eyes?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Yes. Have you not heard Allah Almighty saying: {When it sees them from a far place}? Can it see them except with two eyes?""**"
"654","Abū Qirṣāfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Narrate from me that which you hear, and it is not lawful for any man to lie against me. For whoever lies against me, or attributes to me what I have not said, a house shall be built for him in Hell wherein he shall reside.""**"
"655","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not lie against me, for lying against me is not like lying against anyone else.""**"
"656","Aws ibn Aws رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever lies against his Prophet, or against his two eyes (by claiming false visions), or against his parents (by falsely claiming other lineage), shall not smell the fragrance of Paradise.""**"
"657","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not lie against me; indeed, the one who lies against me is brazenly audacious in sin.""**"
"658","Abū Khaldah reported: I heard Maymūn al-Kurdī while he was in the company of Mālik ibn Dīnār. Mālik ibn Dīnār said to him, ""Why does the elder not narrate from his father, when your father reached the time of the Prophet ﷺ and heard from him?"" Maymūn replied, ""My father would not narrate to us from the Prophet ﷺ out of fear that he might add or omit anything, and he said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:'"" > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"659","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: > **""Three will not smell the fragrance of Paradise: a man who claims lineage from other than his father, a man who lies against his Prophet, and a man who lies against his own eyes (by inventing false dreams).""**"
"660","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is conveyed a hadith from me and denies it, has indeed denied three: Allah, His Messenger, and the one who narrated it.""**"
"661","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is conveyed a virtue promised from Allah and does not believe in it, shall not attain it.""**"
"662","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people in a sermon, saying: > **""How long will you hold back from mentioning the corrupt person? Expose him so that the people may beware of him!""**"
"663","Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah رضي الله عنه also reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no backbiting for a shameless transgressor.""**"
"664","ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah reported: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه sat in a place where the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to sit, where funeral processions would pass by. A funeral passed and the people praised the deceased with good words, so ʿUmar said, ""It has become binding (i.e., Paradise)."" Then another funeral passed, and the people said, ""This man was the greatest liar among people!"" Thereupon ʿUmar رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, the worst of liars among the people is the one who lies against Allah, and then following them are those who lie against the spirit within their own bodies (by fabricating dreams).""**"
"665","Ḥammād ibn Zayd reported: I met Salamah ibn ʿAlqamah and he narrated a hadith to me, then he retracted it and said: > **""If you wish to expose your companion as a liar, then prompt him (with details to see if he falsely claims to know them).""**"
"666","Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf reported: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه sent for Ibn Masʿūd, Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī, and Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنهم, and said: > **""What is this hadith that you narrate in such abundance from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?""** And he detained them in Madinah until he was martyred."
"667","Abū Ḥumayd and Abū Usayd رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you hear a hadith attributed to me which your hearts recognize, towards which your skin and hair soften, and which you perceive to be near to you, then I am the closest of you to it. But when you hear a hadith attributed to me which your hearts reject, from which your hair and skin recoil, and which you perceive to be far from you, then I am the furthest of you from it.""**"
"668","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a hadith is narrated to you from me and it conforms to the truth, then it is I who said it.""**"
"669","Mubārak ibn Faḍālah reported: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm (or Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl) stood up before al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī and said, ""O Abū Saʿīd! We hear hadiths from you that you narrate directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so provide us with their chains of narration!"" Al-Ḥasan replied, ""Ask about whatever you wish."" He asked, ""The hadith of the Prophet ﷺ regarding the establishment of the Hour."" Al-Ḥasan said: > **""Anas ibn Mālik narrated it to me from the Prophet ﷺ; and Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated it to me from the Prophet ﷺ; and ʿAbdullāh ibn Qudāmah—who was a truthful man—narrated it to me from al-Aswad ibn Sarīʿ from the Prophet ﷺ.""** Thereupon they stood up and said, ""We could not prevail over this venerable scholar!"""
"670","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهم reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us with his head wrapped in a cloth, ascended the pulpit, and said: > **""What are these writings that reach me which you are writing down? Is there a book to be kept alongside the Book of Allah? It is near that Allah will become angry for the sake of His Book, so that it will be taken away by night, leaving not a single letter on any leaf or in any heart except that it will be removed.""** One of those present asked, ""Then what will become of the believing men and believing women, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Whoever Allah intends good for, He will preserve in his heart: 'Lā ilāha illā Allāh' (there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah).""**"
"671","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the Children of Israel wrote a book and followed it, and they abandoned the Torah.""**"
"672","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: We were sitting writing down what we heard from the Prophet ﷺ, when he came out to us and asked, ""What is this that you are writing?"" We replied, ""What we hear from you."" He ﷺ said: > **""Is there a book alongside the Book of Allah? Keep the Book of Allah pure and unmixed!""** So we gathered all that we had written in a single open area and burned it with fire. We asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, may we narrate from you?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Yes, narrate from me and there is no harm; and whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** We asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, may we narrate from the Children of Israel?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Yes, narrate from the Children of Israel and there is no harm, for you will not narrate anything concerning them except that there occurred among them what was even more wondrous.""**"
"673","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not write down anything from me except the Qurʾān, and whoever has written down anything from me other than the Qurʾān, let him erase it; and narrate from the Children of Israel, and there is no harm.""**"
"674","Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā reported: I wrote down a document from my father, so he said, ""Were it not that verses from the Book of Allah are in it, I would have burned it!"" He then called for a washbasin or vessel and washed the writing away, saying: > **""Retain in memory what you hear from me and do not write down anything from me, for I never wrote down a book from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. You almost brought ruin upon your father!""**"
"675","Abū Burdah also reported: Whenever I heard a hadith from my father, I wrote it down. He asked, ""O my son, what are you doing?"" I replied, ""I write down what I hear from you."" He said, ""Bring it to me."" So I read it back to him, and he said: > **""Yes, exactly like this did I hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, yet I fear lest something be added to it or omitted from it.""**"
"676","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: > **""There was no one more knowledgeable regarding the hadiths of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than me, except for ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr; for he used to write with his hand and retain with his heart, whereas I used to retain with my heart and did not write with my hand. And he had sought permission from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to write from him, and he ﷺ granted him permission.""**"
"677","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and said: > **""Narrate, and whoever lies against me, let him take his seat in Hell.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we hear things from you; may we write them down?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Write them down, and there is no harm.""**"
"678","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: There were some companions in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I was among them, being the youngest of the group. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever deliberately lies against me, let him take his seat in the Fire.""** When the people departed, I said to them, ""How can you narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when you heard what he said, yet you engage so extensively in narrating from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" They laughed and said, ""O son of our brother! Indeed, everything that we heard from him is preserved with us in writing."""
"679","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: I asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I shackle knowledge?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Yes.""** I asked, ""And what is its shackling?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Writing.""**"
"680","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Shackle knowledge.""** I asked, ""And what is its shackling?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Writing.""**"
"681","Thumāmah reported: Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said to us: > **""Bind knowledge through writing.""**"
"682","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: A man complained to the Prophet ﷺ of poor memory, so he ﷺ said: > **""Seek aid with your right hand (by writing).""**"
"683","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a man complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of poor memory, so he ﷺ said: > **""Seek aid with your right hand to assist your memory (by writing).""**"
"684","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: I used to write down the Divine Revelation for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whenever Revelation descended upon him, severe hardship would seize him, and he would perspire profusely like pearls of sweat. When that state passed from him, I would enter upon him with a shoulder blade or a scrap of leather, and I would write while he dictated to me. Before I could finish, my leg would feel as though it were about to break under the sheer weight of the Qurʾān, until I thought I would never walk upon it again. When I finished, he ﷺ would say: > **""Read it back.""** So I would read it back to him; if there was any omission, he would set it right, and only then would I take it out to the people."
"685","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported: A letter was received by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said to ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam رضي الله عنه: > **""Reply to these people.""** ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam took it and wrote the reply, then brought the letter to present it before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: > **""You have done well.""** ʿUmar said: ""That remained in my heart with admiration until I was appointed caliph, and I placed him in charge of the Public Treasury (Bayt al-Māl)."""
"686","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ employed ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam رضي الله عنه as a scribe, and he would write letters to kings. His trustworthiness in the eyes of the Prophet ﷺ reached such an esteem that he would write to some kings, and the Prophet ﷺ would simply order the clay seal to be placed upon it and stamp it without reading it, out of complete confidence in his trustworthiness. He also employed Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه as a scribe, who recorded the Divine Revelation and also wrote to foreign kings. Whenever ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Arqam and Zayd ibn Thābit were absent and he needed to write to army commanders or rulers, or write a title deed of land allotment for a person, he would command whoever was present to write. Among those who wrote for him were: > **ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, Zayd ibn Thābit, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, Khālid ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and others whom he named from among the Arabs رضي الله عنهم.**"
"687","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Hearing news is not like eyewitness observation. Indeed, Allah Almighty informed Mūsā عليه السلام of what his people had done regarding the calf, yet he did not cast down the Tablets; but when he witnessed with his own eyes what they had done, he cast down the Tablets and they shattered.""**"
"688","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Hearing news is not like eyewitness observation.""**"
"689","Usāmah al-Hudhalī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a community of people—consisting of forty men or more—bears witness, Allah accepts their testimony [or He confirms their testimony].""**"
"690","Ḥumayd reported: We were with Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه when he said: > **""By Allah, not everything that we narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did we hear directly from him; however, none of us ever lied to one another.""**"
"691","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه said: > **""Not all the hadiths did we hear directly from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; rather, his Companions would narrate to us from him, for tending to our camels used to keep us occupied.""**"
"692","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever conveys a hadith exactly as he heard it: if it is good and true, the reward is for you and for him; and if it is a lie, its burden falls upon the one who originated it.""**"
"693","Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Sulaymān ibn Ukaymah al-Laythī reported from his father, from his grandfather رضي الله عنه, who said: We came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""May our fathers and mothers be ransomed for you, O Messenger of Allah! We hear hadiths from you, but we are not able to convey them verbatim as we heard them."" He ﷺ said: > **""As long as you do not make lawful what is forbidden, nor make forbidden what is lawful, and you convey the correct meaning, then there is no harm.""**"
"694","Shaddād reported: Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه used to hear a hadith containing a strict ruling from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, after which he would go out to his people and greet them [conveying it to them]. Later, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would grant a concession regarding it, but Abū Dharr had not heard the concession, so Abū Dharr remained firmly adhered to the initial, stricter command. > **""Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه would hear a strict ruling from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then convey it to his people; subsequently the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would grant a concession regarding it which Abū Dharr had not heard, so Abū Dharr remained steadfastly attached to the stricter ruling.""**"
"695","Al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Muzāḥim reported: Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما passed by a popular story-teller/preacher, nudged him with his foot, and said, ""Do you know what the abrogating (nāsikh) and the abrogated (mansūkh) are?"" The man replied, ""And what are the abrogating and the abrogated?"" Ibn ʿAbbās asked, ""So you do not know what the abrogating and the abrogated are?"" He replied, ""No."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: > **""You are ruined, and you have brought ruin upon others!""**"
"696","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let me not find any one of you, when a hadith from me reaches him while he is reclining on his couch, saying: 'Recite the Qur'an concerning it to me.' Whatever of good reaches you from me—whether I actually uttered it or not—I do indeed say it; and whatever reaches you of evil, then surely I never utter evil.""**"
"697","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It may soon come to pass that a man will deny me while reclining on his couch; a hadith from me will reach him and he will say: 'Whatever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, leave this aside and bring what is in the Qur'an!'""**"
"698","Abū Ḥāzim reported that Sahl رضي الله عنه was in a gathering of his people narrating to them from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while some of them turned toward one another and conversed among themselves. He became angry and said: > **""Look at them! I narrate to them from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what my own eyes saw and my own ears heard, yet they turn toward one another! By Allah, I shall leave from your midst and never return to you.""** I [Abū Ḥāzim] said to him, ""Where will you go?"" He said: > **""I will go and strive in the way of Allah.""** I said, ""How can you fight in Jihad when you cannot stay firm upon a horse, nor can you strike with a sword, nor thrust with a spear?"" He said: > **""O Abū Ḥāzim, I will go and stand in the battle rank, and a stray arrow or a stone will strike me, and Allah will grant me martyrdom.""** The narrator said: By my life, he departed and did not return except mortally wounded."
"699","Khālid ibn al-Walīd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Khālid, announce among the people: 'The congregational prayer is about to begin; none shall enter Paradise except a Muslim soul.'""** Then the Prophet ﷺ came out and led the midday prayer. Afterward, he stood among the people and said: > **""I do not permit taking the property of non-Muslim citizens under covenant (Muʿāhadīn) without due right. It may soon come to pass that a man among you will say while reclining on his couch: 'Whatever we find in the Book of Allah, the Exalted and Glorious, as lawful, we deem lawful; and whatever we find as unlawful, we deem unlawful.' Indeed, I forbid to you the property of the Muʿāhadīn without due right.""**"
"700","Tamīm al-Dārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Every bewildering intoxicant is unlawful, and there is no ambiguity in the religion.""**"
"701","Thawbān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There will soon be factions among my Ummah who will delve into jurisprudence that consists merely of intricate, perplexing dilemmas; they are the worst of my Ummah.""**"
"702","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I wish there were a veil between me and the people of Najrān,""** on account of the severity with which they used to argue with him."
"703","Al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad رضي الله عنه said: I said to the Prophet ﷺ, ""There is something I heard from you concerning which I felt in doubt."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whenever any of you is in doubt regarding a matter, let him ask me about it.""** Al-Miqdād said, ""Your statement regarding your wives: 'I do not anticipate for them after me the truthful ones (al-Ṣiddīqīn).'"" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""And whom do you count as the truthful ones?""** We said, ""Our children who pass away in infancy?"" He ﷺ said: > **""No, the truthful ones are those who give charity (al-mutaṣaddiqūn).""** He repeated this three times."
"704","Abū al-Dardāʾ, Abū Umāmah, Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, and Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنهم reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day while we were disputing concerning a matter of religion. He became intensely angry—with an anger unlike anything we had seen from him before—and rebuked us, saying: > **""Desist, O nation of Muḥammad! Those before you were destroyed only by this. Abandon disputation, for there is little good in it. Abandon disputation, for the believer does not dispute. Abandon disputation, for the disputer’s loss is complete. Abandon disputation, for it is enough of a sin for you to remain disputatious. Abandon disputation, for I will not intercede on the Day of Resurrection for the disputer. Abandon disputation, for I guarantee three houses in Paradise: in its outskirts, in its middle, and in its highest part, for the one who leaves disputation even when he is in the right. Abandon disputation, for the first thing my Lord forbade me from after the worship of idols was disputation and drinking wine. Abandon disputation, for Satan has despaired of being worshipped, but he is pleased with inciting discord among you, and he is the agitator. Abandon disputation, for the Children of Israel split into seventy-one sects, and the Christians split into seventy-two sects, all of them upon misguidance except the Great Majority (al-Sawād al-Aʿẓam).""** They asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the Great Majority?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Whoever adheres to that which I and my Companions are upon; whoever does not dispute in the religion of Allah, nor declares anyone from the people of Tawḥīd to be a disbeliever on account of a sin, he shall be forgiven.""** Then he ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Islam began as something strange, and it will return to being strange.""** They asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, who are the strangers?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Those who remain righteous when the people become corrupt, who do not engage in disputation regarding the religion of Allah, and do not declare anyone from the people of Tawḥīd to be a disbeliever on account of a sin.""**"
"705","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه said: We were sitting at the door of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ discussing amongst ourselves, one citing a verse as evidence and another citing another verse as evidence. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us, his face flushed red as though pomegranate seeds had burst upon it. He said: > **""O you people, is this what you were sent with, or is this what you were commanded to do? Do not revert after me to disbelievers, striking the necks of one another!""**"
"706","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported a narration similar to the preceding hadith from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""O you people, is this what you were sent with, or is this what you were commanded to do? Do not revert after me to disbelievers, striking the necks of one another!""**"
"707","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise, a house in the middle of Paradise, and a house in the highest part of Paradise for the one who abandons disputation even when he is in the right, abandons lying even when joking, and whose character is good.""**"
"708","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise for the one who abandons disputation while being in the right, a house in the middle of Paradise for the one who abandons lying even in jest, and a house in the highest part of Paradise for the one whose inner soul is pure and virtuous.""**"
"709","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Disputation concerning the Qur'an is disbelief.""**"
"710","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not dispute concerning the Qur'an, for indeed disputation concerning it is disbelief.""**"
"711","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No nation ever fell into discord after its Prophet except that its people of falsehood prevailed over its people of truth.""**"
"712","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that ʿĪsā ibn Maryam عليه السلام said: > **""Affairs are indeed of three types: a matter whose right guidance is clear to you, so follow it; a matter whose misguidance is clear to you, so avoid it; and a matter concerning which there is difference, so refer it back to one who possesses knowledge of it.""**"
"713","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sabrah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying: > **""Indeed, Allah forbids you from three things: idle gossip (saying 'it was said' and 'he said'), excessive questioning, and squandering wealth.""**"
"714","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah has disliked for you idle gossip, excessive questioning, and wasting wealth.""**"
"715","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""Counsel me."" He ﷺ said: > **""Abandon idle talk, excessive questioning, and squandering wealth.""**"
"716","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir reported from al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah has disliked for you idle gossip, excessive questioning, withholding rights while demanding from others, burying infant daughters alive, and disobedience to mothers.""**"
"717","When the people questioned him excessively one day, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said to al-Ḥārith ibn Qays: ""O Ḥār ibn Qays, what do you think they intend by what they are asking about?"" Al-Ḥārith replied, ""To learn it and then abandon it."" Ibn Masʿūd said: > **""You have spoken the truth, by the One besides Whom there is no other deity!""**"
"718","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""A people will come who will drink knowledge as one drinks, yet it will not benefit them.""**"
"719","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه said: > **""The people used to ask about things regarding the commands of the Prophet ﷺ; they would question the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning a matter that was lawful, and they would continue asking about it until it was made unlawful for them.""**"
"720","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The verse of mutual imprecation (al-Liʿān) was revealed only because of excessive questioning.""**"
"721","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Leave me alone as long as I leave you alone, for those before you were destroyed only because of their excessive questioning and their disputing with their Prophets. Whatever I command you with, fulfill of it as much as you are able, and whatever I forbid you from, refrain from it.""**"
"722","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Leave me alone as long as I leave you alone, for those before you were destroyed only by their disputing with their Prophets. When I command you with something, fulfill it; and when I forbid you from something, avoid it as much as you are able.""**"
"723","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""I have never seen a people better than the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They did not ask him except about thirteen matters until he was taken, all of which are in the Qur'an: *'They ask you about the sacred month'* [al-Baqarah: 217], *'They ask you about wine and gambling'* [al-Baqarah: 219], *'They ask you about orphans'* [al-Baqarah: 220], *'And they ask you about menstruation'* [al-Baqarah: 222], *'They ask you about the spoils of war'* [al-Anfāl: 1], and *'They ask you what they should spend'* [al-Baqarah: 215]. They did not ask except about that which benefited them.""** He also said: > **""The first to circumambulate the House were the angels. And verily, between the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner lie the graves of Prophets; whenever a Prophet was persecuted by his people, he would depart from their midst and worship Allah there until he passed away.""**"
"724","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whenever the Prophet ﷺ completed the Dawn (Fajr) prayer, we would turn towards him. Some of us would ask him about the Qur'an, some would ask him about the laws of inheritance, and some would ask him about dreams.""**"
"725","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ was sitting in the mosque, and the people thought that revelation was descending upon him, so they held back from him. Then Abū Dharr arrived, stepped forward, and sat beside him. The Prophet ﷺ turned toward him and asked, ""O Abū Dharr, have you prayed today?"" He replied, ""No."" He ﷺ said: > **""Stand and pray.""** When he had prayed four units of the forenoon (Ḍuḥā) prayer, the Prophet ﷺ turned toward him and said: > **""O Abū Dharr, seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the devils among jinn and mankind.""** Abū Dharr asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, are there devils among mankind?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Yes. *'Devils among mankind and jinn, inspiring one another with embellished speech in delusion'* [al-Anʿām: 112].""** Then he ﷺ said: > **""O Abū Dharr, shall I not teach you words from the treasure of Paradise?""** I said, ""Yes indeed, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Say: 'There is no power and no might except with Allah' (*Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh*).""** I said, ""*Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh*."" Abū Dharr said: Then he remained silent, and I eagerly awaited his speech. I said, ""O Prophet of Allah, we were people of Ignorance (Jāhiliyyah) and idol worship, then Allah sent you as a mercy to the worlds. What is prayer?"" He ﷺ said: > **""An established virtue: whoever wills may perform little, and whoever wills may perform much.""** I asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what is fasting?"" He ﷺ said: > **""An obligatory duty that suffices, and with Allah is abundant reward.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, what is charity?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Multiplied manifolds, and with Allah is even more.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, which charity is best?"" He ﷺ said: > **""A secret gift to a pauper, and the exertion of one with scarce means.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, which revelation sent down to you is the greatest?"" He ﷺ said: > **""*'Allah! None has the right to be worshipped but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all'* [al-Baqarah: 255]—Ayat al-Kursī.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, which martyr is superior?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The one whose blood is shed and whose steed is hamstrung.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, which slave is best to emancipate?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The highest in price and most precious to its family.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, which Prophet was the first?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Adam, upon him be peace.""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, was Adam a Prophet?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Yes, a Prophet spoken to; Allah created him with His Hand and breathed into him from His spirit, then said to him: 'O Adam, advance.'""** I asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, how many Prophets were there?"" He ﷺ said: > **""One hundred and twenty-four thousand; and the Messengers among them were three hundred and fifteen, a vast multitude.""**"
"726","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in the mosque and sat down. He said, ""O Abū Dharr, have you prayed?"" I said, ""No."" He said: > **""Stand and pray.""** I stood and prayed, then sat down. He said: > **""O Abū Dharr, seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the devils among mankind and jinn.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, are there devils among mankind?"" He said: > **""Yes.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what of prayer?"" He said: > **""An established virtue: whoever wills may perform little, and whoever wills may perform much.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what of fasting?"" He said: > **""An obligatory duty that suffices, and with Allah is increase.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, what of charity?"" He said: > **""Multiplied manifolds.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, which of it is best?"" He said: > **""The exertion of one with little means, or a secret gift to a pauper.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, which Prophet was first?"" He said: > **""Adam.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, was he a Prophet?"" He said: > **""Yes, a Prophet spoken to.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, how many were the Messengers?"" He said: > **""Three hundred and a dozen or so, a vast multitude.""** And once he said: **""Three hundred and fifteen.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, was Adam a Prophet?"" He said: > **""Yes, spoken to.""** I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, which revelation sent to you is greatest?"" He said: > **""Ayat al-Kursī: *'Allah! None has the right to be worshipped but He, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of all'* [al-Baqarah: 255].""**"
"727","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Moderation in expenditure is half of livelihood, affability toward people is half of wisdom, and asking sound questions is half of knowledge.""**"
"728","Abū Razīn رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to dislike frivolous questions and criticize them; but when Abū Razīn asked him, he answered him and was pleased with it.""**"
"729","Ibrāhīm reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When one of you is in doubt concerning a verse, let him not ask: 'What do you say regarding such-and-such?' lest confusion be cast upon him. Rather, let him recite what precedes it, and then allow him to attend to his need.""**"
"730","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: > **""Whenever the Prophet ﷺ was deeply preoccupied with concern, he would frequently touch his beard.""**"
"731","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: I approached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was on an elevated mound of earth until I sat facing him—or my face was level with his knee. Seizing the opportunity of the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ solitude, I asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, which sin is greatest?"" He turned away from me until I repeated it three times; then he turned his face toward me and said: > **""That you assign a rival to Allah while He alone created you.""**"
"732","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""When the cities were conquered, the people turned toward worldly pursuits, while I turned toward ʿUmar.""** And thus, the general body of his narrations was from ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"733","Abū Firās رضي الله عنه, a man from the tribe of Aslam, reported: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Ask me concerning whatever you wish!""** A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, who is my father?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Your father is so-and-so, to whom you are named.""** Another man asked, ""Am I in Paradise?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""In Paradise.""** Another man asked, ""Am I in Paradise?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""In the Fire.""** Whereupon ʿUmar رضي الله عنه said, ""We are content with Allah as our Lord!"""
"734","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: We were sitting with the Prophet of Allah ﷺ—perhaps around sixty men—and he would narrate hadith to us, then enter his home for some need. We would then review the hadith among ourselves, this one and then that one, until we would stand up as though it had been planted in our hearts: > **""We would review it among ourselves, this one and then that one, and we would stand as though it had been sown into our hearts.""**"
"735","Whenever his companions came to him, Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه would say: > **""Study mutually and rejoice; increase in effort and may Allah increase you in good, love you, and love whoever loves you! Refer questions back to us, for indeed the reward of the last of them is like the reward of the first of them, and intermingle your discourse with seeking forgiveness.""**"
"736","Abū Naḍrah reported: I said to Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه, ""Write down the narrations for us."" He replied: > **""We will not write them down for you, nor will we make them like a Qur'an. Rather, take them from us just as we took them from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ.""** And Abū Saʿīd used to say: > **""Converse with one another in hadith, for indeed one hadith reminds of another.""**"
"737","Kurdūs ibn ʿAmr said: I heard a man from the participants of Badr—Shuʿbah said: ""I believe him to be ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه""—narrating that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""That you expound the detailed narratives (al-Mufaṣṣal) is more beloved to me than such-and-such doors of knowledge.""** Shuʿbah said: I asked ʿAbd al-Malik, ""What is meant by al-Mufaṣṣal?"" He said, ""The historical narratives and lessons."""
"738","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنهما, ""O Abū al-Ḥasan, at times you were present and we were absent, and at times we were present and you were absent. There are three matters I wish to ask you about; do you have knowledge concerning them?"" ʿAlī asked, ""What are they?"" ʿUmar said, ""A man loves another man though he has seen no good from him, and a man detests another though he has seen no evil from him?"" ʿAlī replied, ""Yes; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **'Indeed, souls in the spiritual realm are conscripted soldiers; they meet and scent one another. Those among them that recognized each other become bonded in affection, and those among them that were unacquainted differ in aversion.'**"" ʿUmar said, ""That is one."" ʿUmar asked, ""A man is narrating a hadith when suddenly he forgets it, and then suddenly he remembers it?"" ʿAlī said, ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **'There is no heart except that it has a cloud like the cloud covering the moon. While the moon is shining brightly, a cloud suddenly passes over it and it darkens; when it unveils, it shines again. Likewise, while a man is narrating a hadith, a cloud covers his heart and he forgets; then when it departs, he remembers.'**"" ʿUmar said, ""That is two."" ʿUmar said, ""A man sees dreams, and some of them come true while others prove false?"" ʿAlī said, ""Yes; I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **'No male or female servant falls asleep and enters deep sleep except that their soul is ascended toward the Throne. That soul which does not awaken until it reaches the Throne is the vision that comes true; whereas that which awakens before reaching the Throne is the vision that proves false.'**"" ʿUmar exclaimed, ""These were three matters I was searching for! Praise be to Allah Who enabled me to attain them before death!"""
"739","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked, ""Which of the people is most knowledgeable?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The one who gathers the knowledge of the people unto his own knowledge; and every possessor of knowledge remains hungry for more.""**"
"740","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: I entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and he said, ""O Ibn Masʿūd, which handhold of faith is the firmest?"" I said, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He ﷺ said: > **""The firmest handhold of Islam is loyalty for the sake of Allah, loving for the sake of Allah, and hating for the sake of Allah.""** Then he said, ""O Ibn Masʿūd!"" I said, ""At your service, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said, ""Do you know which of the people is most virtuous?"" I said, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: > **""Verily, the most virtuous of people is the best of them in deeds when they possess deep understanding in their religion.""** Then he said, ""O Ibn Masʿūd!"" I said, ""At your service, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said, ""Do you know which of the people is most knowledgeable?"" I said, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: > **""Verily, the most knowledgeable of people is the most discerning of the truth when people differ, even if he falls short in his deeds, and even if he crawls upon his buttocks. And those before you split into seventy-two sects; three of them were saved and the rest perished: a group that resisted tyrannical rulers and fought them for their religion and the religion of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam ﷺ, so the rulers captured them, killed them, and sawed them with saws; a group that lacked the power to confront the rulers and could not remain among them inviting to the religion of Allah and the religion of ʿĪsā, so they wandered through the land and took up monasticism—and they are those concerning whom Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: *'And monasticism they innovated; We did not prescribe it for them, except seeking the pleasure of Allah'* [al-Ḥadīd: 27]. So whoever believes in me, follows me, and affirms my truth has observed it with its true observance; and whoever does not follow me, then those are the ruined ones.""**"
"741","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is asked concerning knowledge and conceals it will come on the Day of Resurrection bridled with a bit of fire; and whoever speaks concerning the Qur'an without knowledge will come on the Day of Resurrection bridled with a bit of fire.""**"
"742","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever conceals knowledge that he knows will be bridled on the Day of Resurrection with a bit of fire.""** Ibn ʿAbbās explained: ""This refers to the testimony a person holds, whether he is called to give it or not, knowing it yet not directing its rightful owner to it; that is what this knowledge is."""
"743","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is asked about knowledge and conceals it will be bridled on the Day of Resurrection with a bit of fire.""**"
"744","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whichever servant is granted knowledge by Allah and conceals it will meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection bridled with a bit of fire.""**"
"745","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever is asked concerning knowledge and conceals it will be brought on the Day of Resurrection having been bridled with a bit of fire.""**"
"746","Saʿd ibn al-Midḥās رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever knows something, let him not conceal it; and whoever's eyes shed tears out of the fear of Allah, it is not lawful for him to enter the Fire ever, except to fulfill the oath of the Most Merciful; and whoever fabricates a lie against me, let him take his seat in Hellfire.""**"
"747","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of the one who learns knowledge and then does not narrate it is like the likeness of one who hoards a treasure and does not spend from it.""**"
"748","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon one day in which he commended certain groups of Muslims with good words, then said: > **""What is the matter with people who do not impart understanding to their neighbors, do not teach them, do not admonish them, do not command them, and do not forbid them? And what is the matter with people who do not learn from their neighbors, do not acquire understanding, and do not take heed? By Allah, either people will teach their neighbors, impart understanding to them, admonish them, command them, and forbid them, and people will learn from their neighbors, gain discernment, and acquire understanding, or I will surely hasten punishment upon them!""** Then he ﷺ descended from the pulpit. Some people asked, ""Who do you think he meant by these?"" Someone said, ""The Ashʿarīs; they are a people of religious understanding, whereas they have harsh neighbors from the dwellers of the watering springs and desert Bedouins."" When news of this reached the Ashʿarīs, they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you mentioned some people with praise and you mentioned us with reproach; what is our condition?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""A people will surely teach their neighbors, impart understanding to them, enlighten them, command them, and forbid them; and a people will surely learn from their neighbors, gain discernment, and acquire understanding; or I will certainly hasten punishment upon them in this world.""** They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, are we to teach others?"" He repeated his statement to them. They said again, ""Are we to teach others?"" He repeated it again. Then they said, ""Grant us a respite of one year."" So he granted them a respite of one year to impart understanding, teach, and enlighten them. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited this verse: > **""Cursed were those who disbelieved among the Children of Israel by the tongue of David and that of Jesus, the son of Mary...""** [al-Māʾidah: 78]."
"749","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Every building is a burden upon its owner except what is like this—and he gestured with his palm—and all knowledge is a burden upon its possessor on the Day of Resurrection except for the one who acted upon it.""**"
"750","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""O people, learn! And whoever learns, let him put his knowledge into practice.""**"
"751","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is not appropriate for the scholar to remain silent regarding his knowledge, nor is it appropriate for the unlearned to remain silent regarding his lack of knowledge. Allah, Exalted is His remembrance, said: *'So ask the people of the message if you do not know'* [al-Naḥl: 43].""**"
"752","Shuʿbah said: > **""Indeed, this study of hadith diverts you from the remembrance of Allah, from prayer, and from maintaining ties of kinship; so will you not then desist?""**"
"753","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī reported: Those among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who used to teach us recitation narrated to us that they would take ten verses from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they would not proceed to the next ten verses until they mastered whatever knowledge and practical deed was contained within them. He said: > **""Thus, he taught us knowledge and action together.""**"
"754","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها said: I was residing near Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ. Umm Sulaym asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, what is your view if a woman sees in her dream that her husband has intercourse with her, must she perform the ritual bath (Ghusl)?"" Umm Salamah exclaimed, ""May your hands be dusted, O Umm Sulaym! You have embarrassed the women before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ!"" Umm Sulaym responded, ""Indeed, Allah is not shy of the truth, and for us to ask the Prophet ﷺ concerning what perplexes us is better than remaining in blindness regarding it."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""May your hands be dusted, O Umm Sulaym! Ritual bath is obligatory upon her if she finds wetness.""** Umm Salamah asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, does a woman discharge fluid?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Then how else does her child resemble her? Women are indeed the twin halves of men.""**"
"755","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""I have lived a period of my life when one of us would be granted faith before the Qur'an. A Surah would be revealed to Muḥammad ﷺ, and we would learn its lawful and unlawful matters, and where one ought to pause regarding it, just as you learn the Qur'an today. Then later, I have seen men granted the Qur'an before faith; one of them recites from the opening of the Book to its conclusion, not knowing what it commands nor what it forbids, nor where one ought to pause, scattering it like the scattering of inferior dates.""**"
"756","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Abū Mūsā to Yemen and instructed them: > **""Support one another, obey each other, spread glad tidings, and do not repel people.""** Then Muʿādh addressed the people, exhorting them toward Islam, acquiring religious understanding, and reciting the Qur'an. He said: > **""Shall I inform you of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire? When a person is mentioned with good, he is from the people of Paradise; and when he is mentioned with evil, he is from the people of the Fire.""**"
"757","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه said: I saw a thread tied around the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I asked, ""What is this?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""I use it to remind myself.""**"
"758","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه reported: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to tie a thread around his ring to remind himself of matters.""**"
"759","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""People have never given any charity comparable to knowledge that is disseminated.""**"
"760","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Shall I not inform you of the Most Generous of the generous? Allah is the Most Generous of the generous; I am the most generous of the children of Adam; and the most generous of them after me is a man who acquired knowledge and disseminated his knowledge—he will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection as a nation unto himself—and a man who gave his life generously for the sake of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, until he was slain.""**"
"761","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What an excellent gift is a word of truth that you hear and then convey to a Muslim brother of yours, teaching it to him!""**"
"762","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The one who guides to goodness is like the one who does it.""**"
"763","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to a man: > **""Go, for indeed the one who guides to goodness is like the one who does it.""**"
"764","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The one who directs toward goodness is like the one who performs it.""**"
"765","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of you are those who learn the Qur'an and teach it.""**"
"766","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""What an excellent gathering is that in which wisdom is mentioned!""**"
"767","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No man revives and articulates with his tongue a truth that is acted upon after him except that its reward continues to flow for him until the Day of Resurrection; then Allah will grant him his full reward on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"768","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""There are four whose rewards continue to flow for them after death: a man who dies while guarding the frontier in the path of Allah (Murābiṭ); a man who taught knowledge, so its reward flows for him as long as it is acted upon; a man who established an ongoing charity, so its reward is his as long as it continues; and a man who left behind a righteous child who supplicates for him.""**"
"769","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seven deeds continue to yield rewards for a servant after his death while he is in his grave: one who taught knowledge, or excavated a canal, or dug a well, or planted a date-palm, or built a mosque, or bequeathed a copy of the Qur'an (Muṣḥaf), or left behind a child who seeks forgiveness for him after his death.""**"
"770","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said: A man asked for charity during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but the people hesitated. Then a man gave him something, whereupon the rest of the people gave him as well. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever establishes a good precedent (Sunnah) that is followed after him shall have its reward and a share of the rewards of those who follow it, without anything being diminished from their rewards; and whoever establishes an evil precedent that is followed after him shall bear its burden and a share of the burdens of those who follow it, without anything being diminished from their burdens.""**"
"771","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: A delegation from the tribe of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wearing striped woollen garments with openings torn out for their heads, with the marks of severe hardship and poverty evident upon them. What he saw of their distressed state grieved him, so he entered his home and then came out, whereupon he enjoined charity and exhorted the people towards it. Then he ﷺ said: ""Let a man give in charity from a ṣāʿ of his wheat; let him give in charity from a ṣāʿ of his dates."" A man from the Anṣār came forward with a heavy purse of coins and placed it down. Then the people followed one another in succession until a substantial pile of clothing and food had accumulated. Thereupon, the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beamed with joy until it looked as radiant as if it were plated with gold, and he ﷺ said: > **""Whoever initiates a good practice that is acted upon after him shall have its reward and the reward of whoever acts upon it, without diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever initiates an evil practice that is acted upon after him shall bear its burden of sin and the burden of whoever acts upon it, without diminishing anything from their burdens.""**"
"772","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever initiates a good practice shall have its reward as long as it is acted upon during his lifetime and after his death until it is abandoned; and whoever initiates an evil practice shall bear its burden of sin until it is abandoned. And whoever dies stationed as a guard (murābiṭ) in the path of Allah, the reward of the deeds of a frontier guard will continue to flow for him until he is resurrected on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"773","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever calls to guidance and is followed in it shall have the like of the rewards of those who follow him, without diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever calls to misguidance shall bear the like of the sins of those who follow him, without diminishing anything from their sins.""**"
"774","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Indeed, the son of Adam who murdered his brother will surely share half of the torment of the people of the Fire in an exact, equal distribution.""**"
"775","Bishr ibn ʿUbaydillāh—who was an elder of the early generations—narrated: We were with Ṭāwūs at the Maqām (of Ibrāhīm عليه السلام), when he asked, ""What is this?"" Someone replied, ""These are people whom Ibn Hishām seized over a matter and placed iron collars around their necks."" Bishr said: I heard Ṭāwūs narrate from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever introduces an unprecedented evil into this Ummah shall not die until that very affliction befalls him.""** Bishr ibn ʿUbaydillāh said: ""And I indeed saw Ibn Hishām when he was dismissed from office: the deputies of al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik came and placed an iron collar around his neck."""
"776","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما: > **""O young boy! O little lad—[or: O little lad! O young boy!]—preserve from me these words...""** [The narrator then mentioned the rest of the hadith as recorded in the Muʿjam.]"
"777","Thābit al-Bunānī narrated: > **""Whenever I came to visit Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, he would call for perfume and wipe it upon his hands and cheeks.""**"
"778","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us one day as though bidding farewell, and said: > **""I am the unlettered Prophet—repeating it three times—and there is no prophet after me. I have been granted the opening expressions of comprehensive speech and its summaries; I have been taught about the keepers of the Fire and the bearers of the Throne; leniency has been granted through me; and well-being has been granted to me and to my Ummah. So listen and obey as long as I remain among you, and when I am taken away, you must adhere to the Book of Allah: declare lawful its halal, and declare unlawful its haram.""**"
"779","Ibn Shurayḥ al-Khuzāʿī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and said: ""Do you not bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah?"" They replied: ""Yes, indeed."" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, this Qur'an has one of its ends in the Hand of Allah and the other end in your hands. So hold fast to it, for you will never go astray and you will never perish after it ever.""**"
"780","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated: We were with the Prophet ﷺ at al-Juḥfah when he said: ""Do you not bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone without partner, that I am the Messenger of Allah, and that the Qur'an has come from Allah?"" We said: ""Yes, indeed."" He ﷺ said: > **""Then rejoice! For indeed this Qur'an has one of its ends in the Hand of Allah and the other end in your hands. So hold fast to it, for you will never perish and you will never go astray after it ever.""**"
"781","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever follows the Book of Allah, Allah will guide him away from misguidance and protect him from an evil reckoning on the Day of Resurrection; that is because Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, said: *'So whoever follows My guidance shall neither go astray nor fall into misery.'* [Ṭā-Hā: 123]""**"
"782","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Act upon the Qur'an: declare lawful what it permits, declare unlawful what it prohibits, follow its guidance, and do not disbelieve in any part of it. Whatever of it seems unclear to you, refer it back to Allah and to those in authority after me so that they may inform you. Believe in the Torah, the Gospel, the Psalms, and all that was given to the prophets from their Lord. May the Qur'an and the clarity within it heal you, for indeed it is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted, and an advocate whose testimony is confirmed; every verse of it possesses a light until the Day of Resurrection. Verily, I was given Sūrah al-Baqarah from the Primordial Remembrance (al-Dhikr); I was given Ṭā-Hā and al-Ṭūr from the Tablets of Mūsā عليه السلام; I was given the Opening of the Book (al-Fātiḥah) and the concluding verses of Sūrah al-Baqarah from a treasure beneath the Throne; and I was granted the Mufaṣṣal chapters as an extra favor.""** In another narration: ""Whatever of it seems unclear to you, ask the people of knowledge about it, and they will inform you."""
"783","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī narrated from ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنهما, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us during the midday heat in an alarmed state, and said: > **""Obey me as long as I am among you, and adhere to the Book of Allah: declare lawful its halal, and declare unlawful its haram.""**"
"784","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I have left among you two successors: the Book of Allah and my household (Ahl al-Bayt). Verily, the two shall never separate until they meet me at the Basin (al-Ḥawḍ).""**"
"785","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever walks toward Allah's authority on earth intending to humiliate it, Allah will humiliate him by his neck, along with what is stored up for him of punishment in the Hereafter.""** Musaddad added: ""And Allah's authority on earth is the Book of Allah, the Exalted, and the Sunnah of His Prophet ﷺ."""
"786","Thawbān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Behold, the millstone of Islam is turning."" It was asked: ""What should we do, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Examine my hadiths against the Book of Allah: whatever conforms to it is from me and I have said it.""**"
"787","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The Jews were questioned regarding Mūsā عليه السلام, and they exaggerated about him, adding and subtracting until they fell into disbelief; and the Christians were questioned regarding ʿĪsā عليه السلام, and they exaggerated about him, adding and subtracting until they fell into disbelief in him. Soon, traditions attributed to me will spread far and wide. So whatever comes to you of my hadiths, recite the Book of Allah and evaluate it against it: whatever conforms to the Book of Allah, I have indeed said it; and whatever does not conform to the Book of Allah, I did not say it.""**"
"788","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites the Qur'an, standing with it during the hours of the night and day, declaring lawful what it permits and forbidding what it prohibits—Allah will forbid his flesh and blood to the Fire, and make him a companion of the noble and dutiful angel-scribes, until when the Day of Resurrection arrives, the Qur'an will serve as an argument in his favor.""**"
"789","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Muʿādh, indeed the Qur'an restrains the believer from much of the vain desires of his soul.""**"
"790","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the best speech is the Book of Allah, and the best guidance is the guidance of Muḥammad ﷺ. The most evil of matters are newly invented matters, and every innovation is misguidance. Whoever leaves behind wealth, it belongs to his heirs; and whoever leaves behind a debt or dependent family, their care falls upon me.""**"
"791","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, this Qur'an is an intercessor whose intercession is granted. Whoever follows it, it will lead him to Paradise; and whoever abandons it or turns away from it—or words to that effect—he will be shoved by the back of his neck into the Fire.""**"
"792","It was narrated through its chain of transmission from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said the like of it: > **""The Qur'an is an intercessor whose intercession is accepted and an advocate whose testimony is affirmed. Whoever places it before him, it leads him to Paradise; and whoever casts it behind his back, it drives him into the Fire.""**"
"793","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: A group of people were sitting at the door of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disputing about the Qur'an. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them one day with his face visibly altered in displeasure, and said: > **""O people! It was by this that previous nations were destroyed. Indeed, parts of the Qur'an confirm one another, so do not contradict some parts with others!""**"
"794","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whatever Allah has made lawful in His Book is halal, whatever He has forbidden is haram, and whatever He remained silent about is pardoned. So accept from Allah His pardon, for indeed Allah was never one to forget anything."" He ﷺ then recited: *""And your Lord is never forgetful.""* [Maryam: 64]**"
"795","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, Allah has prescribed obligatory duties, so do not neglect them; He has set boundaries, so do not transgress them; and He has remained silent about many things without forgetfulness, so do not burden yourselves seeking them—it is a mercy for you, so accept it.""**"
"796","Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah has ordained obligations, so do not neglect them; He has forbidden things, so do not violate them; He has set limits, so do not transgress them; and He passed over things without forgetfulness, so do not delve into them.""**"
"797","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not attribute anything falsely to me, for indeed I do not declare lawful except what Allah has made lawful in His Book, nor do I declare unlawful except what Allah has made unlawful in His Book.""**"
"798","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon and said: > **""Indeed, Allah has given every possessor of a right his due right. Behold, Allah has prescribed obligatory duties, established sunnahs, set boundaries, made lawful the halal, made unlawful the haram, and legislated the religion, making it easy, accommodating, and expansive, and He did not make it restrictive. Behold, there is no faith for the one who has no trustworthiness, and there is no religion for the one who has no covenant. Whoever breaches Allah's covenant of protection, Allah will demand it of him; and whoever breaches my covenant of protection, I shall be his adversary—and whomever I dispute against, I shall prevail over him. Whoever breaches my covenant of protection shall not attain my intercession nor will he reach the Basin (al-Ḥawḍ). Behold, Allah has not permitted killing except in three cases: an apostate after having believed, an adulterer after having been legally married, or a person who commits murder who is executed in retribution. Behold, have I conveyed the message?""**"
"799","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Sunnah is of two types: a Sunnah regarding an obligatory duty, and a Sunnah regarding what is other than an obligatory duty. The Sunnah regarding an obligatory duty has its foundation in the Book of Allah; adopting it is guidance, and abandoning it is misguidance. And the Sunnah whose foundation is not explicitly in the Book of Allah, adopting it is a virtue, and leaving it is not a sin.""**"
"800","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The one who adheres firmly to my Sunnah at the time of corruption in my Ummah shall have the reward of a martyr.""**"
"801","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Knowledge consists of three things: an articulate Book (the Qur'an), an established Sunnah, and saying: 'I do not know.'""**"
"802","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A time will come upon you in which nothing will be scarcer than three things: a lawfully earned dirham, a loyal brother in whose companionship one finds solace, or a Sunnah that is acted upon.""**"
"803","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه said: The Qur'an was revealed and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ established the Sunnahs. Then ʿImrān said: > **""Follow us (the Companions in our adherence to the Prophet ﷺ), for by Allah, if you do not do so, you will surely go astray!""**"
"804","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Moderation in following a Sunnah is far better than striving hard in an innovation.""**"
"805","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه recounted an event, saying: ""I went and transcribed a text from the People of the Scripture, then brought it written on parchment. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'What is this in your hand, O ʿUmar?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, it is a text I transcribed so that we might increase in knowledge upon our knowledge.' Thereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry until his cheeks turned crimson red. Then the call was proclaimed: 'As-Ṣalātu Jāmiʿah' (Assemble for prayer). The Anṣār said: 'Has your Prophet ﷺ been angered? To arms! To arms!' They came rushing until they surrounded the minbar of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He ﷺ said: > **""O people! Indeed, I have been granted the comprehensive expressions of speech and its conclusions, and it has been condensed for me in supreme brevity. I have brought it to you pure, pristine, and luminous; so do not become bewildered and confounded, and do not let those who are bewildered lead you astray!""** ʿUmar said: ""I stood up and said: 'I am well-pleased with Allah as my Lord, with Islam as my religion, and with you as my Messenger.' Thereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended from the minbar."""
"806","ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I passed by a Jewish brother of mine from Banū Qurayẓah, and he wrote down for me comprehensive excerpts from the Torah. Shall I not present them to you?"" Thereupon, the countenance of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ changed with displeasure. ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit said: I said to ʿUmar: ""Do you not see the expression on the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" So ʿUmar said: ""We are well-pleased with Allah as our Lord, with Islam as our religion, and with Muḥammad ﷺ as our Messenger."" At that, the displeasure lifted from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad! If Mūsā عليه السلام were among you and you were to follow him and abandon me, you would surely go astray. You are my share from among the nations, and I am your share from among the prophets.""**"
"807","ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that ʿUmar transcribed a portion from the Torah, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not ask the People of the Scripture about anything.""**"
"808","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه narrated: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb brought a written text to the Prophet ﷺ that he had acquired from some of the People of the Scripture. He began reading it before the Prophet ﷺ, who became angry and said: > **""Are you confounded and wavering regarding it, O son of al-Khaṭṭāb?! By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I have brought it to you pure and pristine! Do not ask them about anything, lest they inform you of a truth and you deny it, or they inform you of a falsehood and you believe it. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if Mūsā عليه السلام were alive among you, he would have no choice but to follow me.""**"
"809","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه narrated: ʿUmar transcribed a text from the Torah into Arabic and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ. He began reading it while the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ changed with evident displeasure. A man from the Anṣār cried out: ""Woe to you, O son of al-Khaṭṭāb! Do you not see the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not ask the People of the Scripture about anything, for they will never guide you when they have already gone astray; and you will either deny a truth or affirm a falsehood. By Allah, if Mūsā عليه السلام were present among you, it would not be lawful for him except to follow me.""**"
"810","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: ʿUmar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with comprehensive passages from the Torah and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, these are comprehensive passages from the Torah that I obtained from a brother of mine from Banū Zurayq."" The face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ changed with displeasure. Thereupon, ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd—the one who was shown the vision of the Adhān—said: ""Has Allah confounded your intellect? Do you not see what is upon the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" ʿUmar immediately exclaimed: ""We are well-pleased with Allah as our Lord, with Islam as our religion, with Muḥammad as our Prophet, and with the Qur'an as our guide (imām)!"" At that, the displeasure lifted from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad! If Mūsā عليه السلام were among you and you followed him and left me, you would have strayed into deep misguidance. You are my portion among the nations, and I am your portion among the prophets.""**"
"811","Mujāhid narrated: We were traveling with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما on a journey. As he passed by a certain place, he deliberately deviated from the path. He was asked: ""Why did you do that?"" He replied: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do this, so I did it.""**"
"812","Anas ibn Sīrīn narrated: I was with Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما at ʿArafāt. When the time came to depart after midday, I departed with him until the Imam came forward and he prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr with him. Then he stood in supplication along with myself and some companions until the Imam departed, so we departed with him. When he reached the narrow pass before al-Maʾzimayn, he halted his camel and made it kneel down, so we made our mounts kneel, assuming that he wished to pray. His servant who was holding his camel's rein said: > **""He does not intend to pray; rather, he remembered that when the Prophet ﷺ reached this exact spot, he relieved himself, so he loved to relieve himself here as well.""**"
"813","It was reported regarding ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما that he used to visit a specific tree located between Mecca and Medina and take a midday rest under its shade, stating: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to do that.""**"
"814","Zayd ibn Aslam narrated: I saw Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما with his garment buttons unfastened, and he said: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ with his buttons unfastened.""**"
"815","Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad al-Asadī رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask him about righteousness and sin. He ﷺ said: ""Have you come to ask about righteousness and sin?"" I replied: ""By the One Who sent you with the truth, I have not come to ask you about anything else!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Righteousness is that for which your chest expands with tranquility, even if the people give you religious verdicts [permitting it].""**"
"816","Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad al-Asadī رضي الله عنه narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ intending not to leave any question concerning righteousness and sin without asking him about it. Around him was a gathering of Muslims seeking his verdicts. I began making my way through them, so they said: ""Step back from the Messenger of Allah, O Wābiṣah!"" I said: ""Let me draw close to him, for he is the most beloved of people to me to be near!"" He ﷺ said: ""Leave Wābiṣah alone; come close, O Wābiṣah,"" two or three times. So I drew near until I sat directly before him. He asked: ""Shall I inform you, or will you ask me?"" I said: ""No, rather inform me."" He said: ""You have come to ask me about righteousness and sin?"" I replied: ""Yes."" He then tapped his three fingertips against my chest and said: > **""O Wābiṣah, consult your own soul; consult your own soul—three times. Righteousness is that with which the soul is at rest and the heart is at peace, and sin is that which wavers within yourself and hesitates in your chest, even if the people repeatedly give you religious verdicts [to the contrary].""**"
"817","Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī رضي الله عنه narrated: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, inform me of what is lawful for me and what is forbidden to me."" The Prophet ﷺ looked up and down at me attentively, and then said: > **""Righteousness is that with which the soul finds tranquility and the heart finds peace; and sin is that with which the soul does not find tranquility nor does the heart find peace, even if the muftis issue verdicts to you [permitting it].""**"
"818","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ: ""What is sin?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""When something creates unease within yourself, leave it.""** The man asked: ""Then what is faith?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""When your evil deed grieves you and your good deed brings you joy, then you are a true believer.""**"
"819","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Sin is that which agitates and pricks the hearts.""** In another narration: ""That which agitates the chests."" In another narration: ""Whatever glance is cast, Satan finds an ambition in it; and sin is that which agitates the hearts."""
"820","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Six categories of people have I cursed, and Allah has cursed them—and every prophet's supplication is answered: the one who adds to the Book of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime; the one who denies the Divine Decree (Qadar) of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime; the one who violates the sanctities of Allah; the one who deems lawful regarding my family (ʿitrah) what Allah has forbidden; and the one who abandons the Sunnah.""**"
"821","ʿAmr ibn Shaghwā al-Yāfiʿī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Seven types of people have I cursed—and every prophet's supplication is answered: the one who adds to the Book of Allah; the one who denies the Divine Decree of Allah; the one who violates the sanctities of Allah; the one who deems lawful regarding my family what Allah has forbidden; the one who abandons my Sunnah; the one who monopolizes public spoils (fayʾ) for himself; and the tyrant who exercises power unjustly to exalt whom Allah has humiliated and to humiliate whom Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, has exalted.""**"
"822","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, the one who declares the lawful to be forbidden is like the one who declares the forbidden to be lawful.""**"
"823","Umm Maʿbad, the freed-maid of Qaraẓah ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنها, said: > **""O my son! Indeed, the one who declares forbidden what Allah has made lawful is like the one who declares lawful what Allah has made forbidden.""**"
"824","ʿAbdah al-Suwāʾī رضي الله عنه narrated: A group of people were making noise and commotion near the Prophet ﷺ, so his Companions said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if only you would send someone to them to forbid them from this."" He ﷺ said: > **""If I were to send someone to forbid them from going to al-Ḥajūn, some of them would surely go there even if they had no need to do so.""**"
"825","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated one day while in front of him people were acting in a manner he disliked—of their chatter and clamor. It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will you not forbid them?"" He ﷺ said: > **""If I were to forbid them from al-Ḥajūn, one of them would likely rush to it even though he has no need for it.""**"
"826","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""A person might say: 'Indeed, Allah commanded such-and-such or forbade such-and-such,' and Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, will say to him: 'You have lied!' Or he might say: 'Indeed, Allah forbade such-and-such and permitted such-and-such,' and Allah will say to him: 'You have lied!'""**"
"827","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, the one who declares the lawful to be forbidden is like the one who declares the forbidden to be lawful.""**"
"828","Ṣuhayb رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""He has not truly believed in the Qur'an who deems lawful its prohibitions.""**"
"829","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported: A man once said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: ""When the Confederates (al-Aḥzāb) arrive, forbid the people of Medina from watering the date palms."" The Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""If I were to forbid you, you would be consumed by destruction; for indeed, the mountains themselves cannot bear the weight of what the prophets declare forbidden.""**"
"830","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Two are better than one, three are better than two, and four are better than three. So adhere to the main body (al-Jamāʿah), for indeed Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, will never unite my Ummah except upon guidance.""**"
"831","Abū Baṣrah رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I asked my Lord, the Mighty and Sublime, for four things, and He granted me three and withheld one from me: I asked Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, not to unite my Ummah upon misguidance, and He granted it to me.""**"
"832","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, looked into the hearts of His servants and found the heart of Muḥammad ﷺ to be the best of all hearts, so He chose him for Himself and sent him with His messages. Then He looked into the hearts of His servants after the heart of Muḥammad ﷺ, and found the hearts of his Companions to be the best of the hearts of servants, so He made them the ministers of His Prophet ﷺ, fighting for His religion. Therefore, whatever the Muslims view as good is good in the sight of Allah, and whatever the Muslims view as bad is bad in the sight of Allah.""**"
"833","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is your instruction if an affair confronts us regarding which no Qur'an has been revealed and no Sunnah from you has been established?"" He ﷺ said: > **""You shall make it a matter of mutual consultation (shūrā) among the devout worshippers from among the believers, and do not decide it based on individual opinion alone.""** [The narrator then mentioned the rest of the hadith, which appears in full in the chapter on Qiyās.]"
"834","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if an affair befalls us in which there is no explicit command or prohibition, what do you instruct me to do?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Consult concerning it the jurists (fuqahāʾ) and the devout worshippers, and do not enforce regarding it the personal opinion of an individual.""**"
"835","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, a man of Quraysh possesses twice the strength of a man from other than Quraysh.""** The sub-narrator said: I asked al-Zuhrī: ""What did he mean by that?"" He replied: ""Soundness and nobility of judgment."""
"836","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to dispatch Muʿādh to Yemen, he consulted a group of his Companions, among whom were Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, ʿAlī, Ṭalḥah, al-Zubayr, and Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنهم. He sought their advice, and Abū Bakr said: ""Were it not that you asked our counsel, we would not have spoken."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""In matters regarding which revelation has not been sent down to me, I am like any one of you."" The people then spoke, each presenting his viewpoint. The Prophet ﷺ asked: ""What do you think, O Muʿādh?"" I said: ""I hold the view expressed by Abū Bakr."" Thereupon, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah above His heavens dislikes that Abū Bakr should make an error.""**"
"837","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was walking among the date-palm groves in Medina, and the people began estimating: ""There is a wasq of fruit here."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is such-and-such an amount."" A man said: ""Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth."" Thereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I am only a human being like you. Whatever I narrate to you from Allah is absolute truth; but whatever I say regarding worldly matters from my own judgment, I am only a human being—I may hit the mark or I may err.""**"
"838","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whatever I inform you is from Allah, it is that concerning which there is no doubt whatsoever.""**"
"839","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed by people cross-pollinating date-palms and said: ""I do not think this avails anything."" So they refrained from doing it that year, and the dates yielded unfertilized, shriveled fruit (shayṣ). When the Prophet ﷺ was informed of this, he said: > **""You know best what is conducive to your affairs in your worldly life.""**"
"840","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما raised it to the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""There is no one except that his statements may be accepted or rejected, except for the Prophet ﷺ.""**"
"841","ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""My Ummah will divide into seventy-odd sects. The greatest of them in terms of trial (fitnah) upon my Ummah will be a people who evaluate matters according to their personal opinions, thereby declaring lawful what is forbidden and declaring forbidden what is lawful.""**"
"842","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""This Ummah will act for an era according to the Book of Allah; then it will act for an era according to the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; and then it will act for an era according to personal opinion. When they act upon personal opinion, they will have gone astray and led others astray.""**"
"843","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه said: > **""Be suspect of your own opinion when it comes to religion! For indeed, I saw myself contesting the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while intending nothing but the truth, and that was on the day of Abū Jandal. The treaty document was before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the delegates of Mecca. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Write: In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.' They replied: 'Do you suppose that we have affirmed your claim? Rather, write: In Your Name, O Allah.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ agreed, while I vehemently objected against them, until he said to me: 'O ʿUmar, do you see that I have consented yet you refuse?' I replied: 'Then I too have consented.'""**"
"844","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Battle of Ḥunayn, there was revealed to him: *""When the victory of Allah has come and the conquest...""* [al-Naṣr: 1], to the end of the narrative. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib! O Fāṭimah bint Muḥammad! The help of Allah and the conquest have come, and I have seen the people entering into the religion of Allah in multitudes. So glory be to my Lord and praise be to Him, and I ask His forgiveness; indeed, He is ever-accepting of repentance. And O ʿAlī, indeed after me there will be struggle among the believers."" ʿAlī asked: ""Upon what basis shall we fight believers who say, 'We have believed in Allah'?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Against the innovations introduced into religion, when they act upon personal opinion; and there is no personal opinion in religion—the religion is only from the Lord: His commands and His prohibitions.""** ʿAlī said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is your instruction if an affair confronts us regarding which no Qur'an has been revealed and no Sunnah from you has been established?"" The Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""You shall make it a matter of mutual consultation (shūrā) among the devout worshippers from among the believers, and do not decide it based on individual opinion alone. If I were to appoint anyone as a successor, none would have been more entitled than you—due to your early acceptance of Islam, your kinship to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, your being his son-in-law, having with you the Leader of the Women of the Believers, and before that, the trials Abū Ṭālib bore for my sake while the Qur'an was being revealed, so I am eager to honor him through his son.""**"
"845","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The affairs of the Children of Israel remained upright and balanced until the offspring of foreign captives emerged among them, who issued religious verdicts based on personal opinion, thereby going astray and leading others astray.""**"
"846","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Soon you will see devils among mankind; one of them hears a hadith and then measures it by analogy against something else, thereby misleading the people away from listening to it from its companion who narrates it.""**"
"847","Al-Shaʿbī reported: Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Beware of saying, 'What do you think? What do you think?' (speculative hypotheticals), for those who came before you were only ruined by saying, 'What do you think? What do you think?' And do not measure one thing against another by analogy, lest a foot slip after having been firmly placed. So when one of you is asked about that which he does not know, let him say, 'I do not know,' for indeed that is one-third of knowledge.""**"
"848","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""I do not measure anything against another by analogy, lest a foot slip after having been firmly placed.""**"
"849","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""There is no year except that the one following it is worse than it; not that one year is inherently better than another, nor one people better than another, but rather through the passing away of your scholars and the best among you. Then a people will arise who judge matters by their own opinions, thereby demolishing and breaching the edifice of Islam.""**"
"850","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""None of you should ever blindly imitate a man in his religion, such that if he believes, he believes, and if he disbelieves, he disbelieves. But if you must follow someone, then follow the dead, for indeed the living is not safe from fitnah (trial and temptation).""**"
"851","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: ""None of you should ever be an *immaʿah* (a spineless conformist)."" They asked, ""And what is an *immaʿah*, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?"" He replied: > **""It is for you to say, 'I am only with the people: if they are guided, I am guided, and if they go astray, I go astray.' Beware! Let every one of you steel himself that even if all the people were to disbelieve, he would not disbelieve.""**"
"852","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The people most severely punished on the Day of Resurrection are: a man who killed a prophet or was killed by a prophet, a man who misleads people without knowledge, or an image-maker who makes statues.""**"
"853","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Follow and do not innovate, for indeed you have been sufficiently provided for.""**"
"854","ʿAmr ibn Salamah reported: We were sitting at the door of Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ when Abū Mūsā [al-Ashʿarī] رضي الله عنه arrived and said, ""Come out to us, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān!"" So Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه came out, and Abū Mūsā said, ""By Allah, nothing brought me out at this hour except that I witnessed a matter that alarmed me, though it appeared good, yet it alarmed me, though it appeared good: a group of people sitting in the mosque, and a man saying, 'Say *Subḥānallāh* such-and-such times, say *Al-ḥamdu lillāh* such-and-such times!'"" ʿAmr said: So ʿAbdullāh departed and we went along with him until he came upon them. He said: > **""How swiftly you have rushed into error while the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ are still alive, his wives are still among you, and his garments and utensils have not even decayed! Count your evil deeds, for I guarantee on behalf of Allah that He will not let any of your good deeds be lost!""**"
"855","Abū al-Bakhtarī reported: Word reached ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that a group of people were sitting between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ saying, ""Say such-and-such, say such-and-such."" ʿAbdullāh said, ""If they do so, notify me."" When they gathered, people came to him, so he went with them and sat down wearing a hooded cloak (*burnus*). When they began their chanting of glorification, ʿAbdullāh uncovered his head from the cloak and said, ""I am ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd!"" The people fell silent. He said: > **""You have indeed brought forth an unjust innovation, or else have you surpassed in guidance the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ?!""** ʿAmr ibn ʿUtbah ibn Farqad said, ""I seek the forgiveness of Allah, O Ibn Masʿūd, and I turn to Him in repentance."" So he ordered them to disperse. And Ibn Masʿūd once saw two circles in the mosque of Kufa, so he stood between them and asked, ""Which of you was formed before the other?"" One of them said, ""We were."" He said to the other, ""Stand up and join them,"" and he made them into a single circle. In another narration, Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""If you adhere to the path of the people (the Companions), you will have outstripped everyone by a far and manifest margin; but if you take to the right and left, you will have strayed into far-reaching misguidance.""**"
"856","Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd reported: Whenever my father (Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه) prayed in the mosque, he would keep it concise while completing the bowing and prostrations properly; and whenever he prayed at home, he would lengthen the bowing, prostrations, and the prayer. I said, ""O my father, when you pray in the mosque you keep it concise, and when you pray at home you lengthen it?"" He replied: > **""O my son, indeed we are leaders who are taken as examples to be followed.""**"
"857","Khālid ibn ʿUrfuṭah reported: I was sitting with ʿUmar [ibn al-Khaṭṭāb] رضي الله عنه when a man from ʿAbd al-Qays whose residence was in Sūs was brought to him. ʿUmar said to him, ""Are you so-and-so, son of so-and-so al-ʿAbdī?"" He said, ""Yes."" So ʿUmar struck him with a stick he had. The man said, ""What is my offense, O Commander of the Faithful?"" ʿUmar said to him, ""Sit down."" He sat down, and ʿUmar recited to him: *{Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm. Alif-Lām-Rā. Those are the verses of the clear Book. Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur'an that you might understand. We relate to you the best of stories in what We have revealed to you of this Qur'an, though you were before it among the unaware}* [Yūsuf: 1-3]. He recited it to him three times and struck him three times. The man asked, ""What is my offense, O Commander of the Faithful?"" ʿUmar said, ""Are you the one who copied the books of Daniel?"" The man replied, ""Command me with your order and I shall follow it."" ʿUmar said, ""Go and efface it with hot water and white wool, and never read it yourself nor teach it to anyone among the people; for if it reaches me that you read it or had anyone read it, I shall severely punish you!"" Then ʿUmar said to him, ""Sit down."" He sat before him, and ʿUmar said: ""I once went and transcribed a book from the People of the Scripture, and I brought it inscribed upon leather. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, 'What is this in your hand, O ʿUmar?' I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, a book I copied to add knowledge to our knowledge.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became so angry that his cheeks flushed red. Then the call was made: *Al-Ṣalātu Jāmiʿah* (The prayer gathers us). The Anṣār said, 'Has your Prophet ﷺ been angered? To arms! To arms!' They came and surrounded the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He ﷺ said: > **""O people, indeed I have been given the most comprehensive words and their conclusions, and matters have been summarized for me concisely. And I have brought it to you pure and white, so do not become bewildered, and let not the confused deceive you!""** ʿUmar said, ""I stood up and said: 'I am well-pleased with Allah as my Lord, with Islam as my religion, and with you as my Messenger!' Thereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped down."""
"858","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said: > **""By Allah, if I wished, I could narrate to you a thousand statements for which you would love me, follow me, and believe me, considering it righteousness from Allah and His Messenger; and if I wished, I could narrate to you a thousand statements for which you would hate me, shun me, and deem me a liar.""**"
"859","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, the one who issues fatwas to the people on every single matter they ask him about is mad.""**"
"860","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah—Mighty and Majestic is He—cannot be overcome, nor deceived, nor informed of that which He does not know. Whomever Allah intends good for, He grants him understanding in religion; and whomever He does not grant understanding, He pays no regard to him.""**"
"861","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, let not a time overtake me—or may you not reach a time—wherein the knowledgeable scholar is not followed, nor is modesty shown before the forbearing; their hearts are the hearts of non-Arabs while their tongues are the tongues of Arabs.""**"
"862","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There was a pregnant dog among the Children of Israel. A guest visited her owners, so she thought: 'I will not bark at the guest tonight.' But the puppies in her womb whimpered. Thereupon it was revealed to a man among them: 'The likeness of this female dog is like a nation that will come after you, whose foolish will overpower their scholars.'""**"
"863","ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr رضي الله عنه said: I have heard a hadith since long ago: > **""When you are among a people of twenty men—fewer or more—and you scrutinize their faces, yet you do not see among them a single man who is held in awe for the sake of Allah—Mighty and Majestic—then know that the matter has indeed grown frail.""**"
"864","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever acquires knowledge to boast before the scholars, to dispute with the foolish, or to attract people's attention to himself, he is in the Fire.""**"
"865","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever acquires knowledge to boast before the scholars or to dispute with the foolish, he is in the Fire.""**"
"866","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever seeks knowledge to boast before the scholars or to dispute with the foolish in gatherings will not even perceive the fragrance of Paradise.""**"
"867","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥusayn reported that it reached him that Luqmān the Wise عليه السلام used to say: > **""O my son, do not learn knowledge to boast before the scholars, nor to argue with the foolish, nor to show off with it in gatherings.""**"
"868","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the likeness of knowledge that does not benefit is like a treasure from which nothing is spent in the path of Allah.""**"
"869","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of the one who teaches people good while forgetting himself is like the wick of a lamp: it illuminates for the people while burning itself.""**"
"870","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Many a bearer of understanding (fiqh) is not truly a jurist, and whomever his knowledge does not benefit, his ignorance harms him. Recite the Qur'an as long as it deters you from sin; for if it does not deter you, then you are not truly reciting it.""**"
"871","Abū Tamīmah reported: I set out with Jundub ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Azdī رضي الله عنه, the Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, toward Basra until we arrived at a place called Bayt al-Miskīn, which is at a distance of about a day's travel from Basra. He asked, ""Did you use to teach anyone the Qur'an?"" I replied, ""Yes."" He said, ""When we reach Basra, bring them to me."" So I brought him Ṣāliḥ ibn Musarriḥ, Abū Bilāl and his grandfather, and Nāfiʿ ibn al-Azraq—and in my estimation back then, they were among the finest people of Basra. He then began narrating to me from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Jundub said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The likeness of the scholar who teaches people good while forgetting himself is like a lamp that illuminates for the people while burning itself. And let none of you be barred from Paradise while looking at its gates on account of a handful of blood shed unjustly.""** Abū Tamīmah said: The people then spoke, mentioning commanding good and forbidding evil, while Jundub remained silent listening to them. Then he said, ""I have never seen people who are more deserving of salvation like these people today—if only they remain truthful!"""
"872","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The most severely punished of people on the Day of Resurrection is a scholar whom his knowledge does not benefit.""**"
"873","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to a clan of Qays to teach them the laws of Islam. I found them to be like wild camels, with distracted gazes, having no concern except for sheep or camels. So I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said, ""O ʿAmmār, what did you do?"" So I related to him the story of the people and informed him of their heedlessness. He ﷺ said: > **""O ʿAmmār, shall I not inform you of people even more astonishing than them? A people who know what those were ignorant of, yet remain heedless just as they were heedless.""**"
"874","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: I approached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was circumambulating the House (the Kaʿbah), and I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, which of the people is the worst?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""O Allah, grant forgiveness! Ask about good and do not ask about evil."" He then said: > **""The worst of people are the evil scholars among the people.""**"
"875","Al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, some people from the dwellers of Paradise will go to some people among the dwellers of the Fire and say, 'Why have you entered the Fire? By Allah, we did not enter Paradise except through what we learned from you!' They will reply, 'Indeed, we used to say it, but we did not act upon it.'""**"
"876","Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Religion will prevail until merchants travel far and wide and seas are traversed in the cause of Allah. Then, after you, there will come peoples who will recite the Qur'an and say: 'We have recited the Qur'an; who is more proficient in reciting than us? Who is more knowledgeable than us? Who possesses deeper understanding than us?'"" Then he ﷺ turned to his Companions and said, ""Is there any good in those people?"" They replied, ""No."" He ﷺ said: > **""Those are from you, from this Ummah, and those are the fuel of the Fire!""**"
"877","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Islam will prevail until merchants sail across the sea and horses charge forth in the path of Allah. Then a people will appear who will recite the Qur'an and say: 'Who recites better than us? Who knows more than us? Who has deeper understanding than us?'"" Then he ﷺ asked his Companions, ""Is there any good in those?"" They replied, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He ﷺ said: > **""Those are from you, from this Ummah, and those are the fuel of the Fire!""**"
"878","Umm al-Faḍl and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهم reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up during the night in Mecca and said three times: ""O Allah, have I conveyed the message?"" Thereupon ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه—who was tender-hearted and devoted—stood up and said, ""O Allah, yes! You have exhorted, striven, and advised sincerely."" When morning came, he ﷺ said: > **""Faith will surely prevail until disbelief is driven back to its homelands, and the seas will be traversed with Islam. And there will surely come upon people a time in which they learn the Qur'an—they will learn it and recite it, and say: 'We have recited and we have known, so who is better than us?' Will there be any harm in those?"" They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, who are they?"" He ﷺ said: ""Those are from you, and those are the fuel of the Fire!""**"
"879","Mujāhid reported from Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, tracing it back to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""Whoever says, 'Indeed, I am a scholar,' then he is ignorant.""**"
"880","Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr said: > **""Whoever says, 'Indeed, I am a scholar,' is ignorant; and whoever says, 'Indeed, I am ignorant,' is ignorant; and whoever says, 'Indeed, I am in Paradise,' is in the Fire; and whoever says, 'Indeed, I am in the Fire,' is in the Fire.""**"
"881","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I fear three things for you, and they will surely come to pass: the slip of a scholar, the disputation of a hypocrite using the Qur'an, and worldly wealth that will be opened up for you.""**"
"882","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beware of three things: the slip of a scholar, the disputation of a hypocrite using the Qur'an, and worldly allure that severs your necks. As for the slip of a scholar: if he is rightly guided, do not blindly imitate him in your religion; and if he slips, do not sever your hopes from him. As for the disputation of a hypocrite using the Qur'an: indeed, the Qur'an possesses signposts like the beacons along a path, so whatever you recognize as familiar, take it, and whatever you find objectionable, refer it back to its knowledgeable scholar. And as for the worldly life that severs your necks: whoever Allah places contentment within his heart, he is truly wealthy.""**"
"883","ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, I fear for my nation three things: the slip of a scholar, a followed passion, and an unjust ruler's decree.""**"
"884","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I do not fear for my nation a believer nor a polytheist. As for the believer, his faith holds him back; and as for the polytheist, his disbelief restrains him. Rather, I fear for you the hypocrite who is learned of tongue: he speaks that which you approve, but acts in ways that you condemn.""**"
"885","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the most frightening of what I fear for you after me is every hypocrite who is eloquent and learned of tongue.""**"
"886","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned us against every hypocrite who is learned and eloquent of tongue.""**"
"887","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I fear for my nation two things: the Qur'an and milk. As for milk, they will pursue fertile countryside, indulge in worldly lusts, and abandon the prayer. And as for the Qur'an, the hypocrites will learn it in order to argue with those who believe.""**"
"888","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What I fear most for my nation after me is a man who misinterprets the Qur'an, placing it in contexts other than its proper places, and a man who believes that he is more entitled to this authority of rule than anyone else.""**"
"889","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There will come upon my nation a time when the reciters will be many and the jurists (fuqahāʾ) will be few; knowledge will be taken away, and turmoil (harj) will increase."" They asked, ""And what is harj?"" He replied: ""Killing amongst yourselves. Then after that, a time will come when men will recite the Qur'an, but it will not go beyond their collarbones. Then a time will come when the hypocrite and the polytheist will argue against the believer.""**"
"890","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, what I fear most for you is a man who recites the Qur'an until its radiance is seen upon him and he becomes a supporter of Islam; then he separates from it to whatever extent Allah wills, turns against his neighbor with his sword, and accuses him of polytheism (shirk).""**"
"891","Abū Barzah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I only fear for you the seductive lusts of deviation in your bellies and private parts, and the misleading whims of passion.""**"
"892","Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith al-Thumālī رضي الله عنه said: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān sent for me and said, ""O Abū Sulaymān, we have united the people upon two practices."" He asked, ""And what are they?"" ʿAbd al-Malik said, ""Raising hands on the pulpits on the day of Jumuʿah, and delivering admonishing stories after Fajr and ʿAṣr."" Ghuḍayf replied, ""Indeed, both of them are innovations according to me, and I will not comply with you in either of them."" ʿAbd al-Malik asked, ""Why?"" He said, ""Because the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No people introduce an innovation except that an equivalent amount of the Sunnah is lifted; therefore, clinging to a Sunnah is better than introducing an innovation.""**"
"893","Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith al-Thumālī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No nation ever introduces an innovation into its religion after its Prophet except that it forfeits an equivalent amount of the Sunnah.""**"
"894","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""No year comes upon the people except that they introduce an innovation in it and extinguish a Sunnah, until innovations become alive and the Sunnahs die.""**"
"895","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no deity worshipped beneath the shadow of the heavens besides Allah that is greater in offense before Allah than a followed lust or whim.""**"
"896","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها: > **""O ʿĀʾishah, indeed those who divide their religion and break into sects are the people of innovations and the people of base desires. There is no repentance for them; I am free of them, and they are free of me.""**"
"897","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever walks to an innovator to honor him has indeed aided in the demolition of Islam.""**"
"898","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUmayr al-Thumālī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The most dreadful matter, the spine-breaking burden, and the unrelenting evil is the manifestation of religious innovations.""**"
"899","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""My nation will split into seventy-three sects; all of them will be in the Fire except one."" They asked, ""And which sect is that?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""That which I and my Companions are upon today.""**"
"900","Khabbāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, when the Children of Israel were ruined, they took to storytelling.""**"
"901","Al-Ḥārith ibn Muʿāwiyah reported: He rode to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه to ask him about three matters. He arrived in Medina, and ʿUmar asked him, ""What brought you?"" He replied, ""To ask you about three matters."" ʿUmar asked, ""And what are they?"" He said, ""Sometimes I and a woman are in a cramped structure when the time for prayer arrives; if I pray with her alongside me, she is next to me, but if she prays behind me, she would be outside the building."" ʿUmar said, ""Screen between yourself and her with a garment, then she may pray alongside you if you wish."" He asked, ""And regarding the two units of prayer after ʿAṣr?"" ʿUmar said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade me from them."" He asked, ""And regarding delivering admonishing stories (al-qaṣaṣ)?"" ʿUmar said, ""Whatever you wish""—as if he disliked forbidding him outright. Al-Ḥārith said, ""I only wished to abide by your verdict."" ʿUmar said: > **""I fear for you that you will preach and grow proud in yourself; then you will preach again and grow even prouder in yourself, until it appears to you that you are above them at the station of the Pleiades (al-Thurayyā), whereupon Allah will place you beneath their feet on the Day of Resurrection to that exact extent.""**"
"902","Abū Ṣāliḥ Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ghifārī reported: Sulaym ibn ʿUtr al-Tujībī was delivering stories and admonitions to the people while standing, so Ṣilah ibn al-Ḥārith al-Ghifārī رضي الله عنه—who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ—said to him: > **""By Allah, we did not abandon the covenant of our Prophet, nor did we sever the ties of our kinship, until you and your companions began standing up among us!""**"
"903","ʿAmr ibn Zurārah said: ʿAbdullāh—meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه—stood over me while I was preaching stories, and said: > **""O ʿAmr, you have either introduced an innovation of misguidance, or you are more rightly guided than Muḥammad ﷺ and his Companions! And indeed I saw them dispersing away from me until I saw not a single person left in my place.""**"
"904","Yaḥyā al-Bakkāʾ reported: Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما saw a preacher in al-Masjid al-Ḥarām while his son was with him. His son asked him, ""What is this man saying?"" He replied: > **""This one is merely saying: 'Notice me! Notice me! (Recognize my status!)'""**"
"905","ʿAmr ibn Dīnār reported: Tamīm al-Dārī رضي الله عنه asked permission from ʿUmar رضي الله عنه to deliver admonishing stories, but ʿUmar refused to grant him permission. He asked again, and ʿUmar refused. He asked yet again, whereupon ʿUmar said: > **""If you so wish""—and he gestured with his hand toward his throat, indicating that it is slaughter.**"
"906","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه reported: > **""Storytelling was not practiced during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, nor during the Caliphate of Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه. The first person to deliver stories was Tamīm al-Dārī رضي الله عنه, who asked permission from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه to deliver stories to the people standing, and ʿUmar granted him permission.""**"
"907","ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Khawlānī reported: A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque and found Kaʿb preaching stories. He asked, ""Who is this?"" They replied, ""Kaʿb, delivering stories."" The Companion said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""None delivers admonishing stories except a ruler, an authorized appointee, or an arrogant show-off.""** He said: When that reached Kaʿb, he was never seen delivering stories thereafter."
"908","ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""None delivers stories except a ruler, an authorized appointee, or a pretentious intruder.""**"
"909","Kaʿb ibn ʿIyāḍ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The storytellers are three: a ruler, an authorized appointee, or an arrogant show-off.""**"
"910","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""None delivers stories except a ruler, an authorized appointee, or a pretentious intruder.""**"
"911","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon a preacher who was admonishing the people, whereupon the preacher paused. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Continue your admonition! For indeed, that I should sit from the morning prayer until the sun rises is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves; and that I should sit after ʿAṣr until the sun sets is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.""**"
"912","A man from the veterans of Badr رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""That I should sit in a gathering like this is more beloved to me than freeing four slaves.""** (Shuʿbah said: I asked, ""What gathering did he mean?"" He replied: ""It was a gathering of admonition and preaching."")"
"913","Kurdūs ibn ʿAmr reported: I heard a man from the veterans of Badr—Shuʿbah said: I think he was ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه—narrating that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""That we elucidate the detailed discourses is more beloved to me than such-and-such matters.""** (Shuʿbah said: I asked ʿAbd al-Malik, ""Which detailed discourse?"" He replied: ""The admonishing stories."")"
"914","The four ʿAbdullāhs—ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAbbās, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهم—reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The story-teller awaits detestation, the listener awaits mercy, the honest trader awaits provision, the hoarder awaits a curse, and the wailing woman along with the women surrounding her—upon them is the curse of Allah, the angels, and all mankind.""**"
"915","Al-Shaʿbī reported: ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said to Ibn Abī al-Sāʾib, the preacher of the people of Medina: ""There are three things concerning which you must comply with me, or else I will certainly oppose you."" He said, ""What are they, O Mother of the Believers? Rather, I shall obey you."" She said: > **""Avoid rhymed prose (sajʿ) in supplication, for indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his Companions did not use to do that. And preach to the people once every week; if you refuse, then twice; and if you insist, then at most thrice, so that you do not cause the people to grow bored of this Book. And do not let me ever find you approaching people while they are engaged in their conversation and interrupting them; rather, leave them be, and when they seek it from you and ask you for it, then speak to them.""**"
"916","Al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you speak to the people, do not narrate to them that which terrifies them or burdens them heavily.""**"
"917","Al-Aʿmash reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه passed by a man admonishing a group of people, so he said: > **""O exhorter, do not cause the people to despair of Allah's mercy!""**"
"918","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Do not bore the people, lest they grow weary of the remembrance.""**"
"919","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Narrate from the Children of Israel, for indeed among them were wondrous events.""**"
"920","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to narrate to us about the Children of Israel for the greater part of the night, not rising except for the majesty and importance of the prayer.""**"
"921","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his companions: > **""Indeed, Allah took the soul of Dāwūd (David) عليه السلام, and his followers neither succumbed to tribulation nor altered their religion; and the companions of al-Masīḥ (Jesus) عليه السلام remained upon his guidance and sunnah for two hundred years.""**"
"922","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beware of religious excess and vain pride, for many among the Children of Israel indulged in excess, to the extent that a short woman would take two wooden shoes, pad them, insert her feet into them, and then deliberately walk beside a tall woman so that she would appear equal in height to her, or even taller than her.""**"
"923","Abū al-Zaʿrāʾ reported: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Do not ask the People of the Scripture about anything, for they will never guide you when they have already led themselves astray. Otherwise, they may tell you something true and you would deny it, or they may tell you something false and you would believe it.""**"
"924","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the Children of Israel wrote a book and followed it, abandoning the Torah.""**"
"925","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There was a Prophet among the Prophets who used to draw lines (divinely revealed geomancy). So whosesoever line coincides with his line, then he has attained knowledge.""**"
"926","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported—Sufyān said: ""I know it to be only from the Prophet ﷺ""—regarding the verse: *{...or a remnant of knowledge}* [al-Aḥqāf: 4]: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""It is the drawing of lines (al-khaṭṭ).""**"
"927","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the drawing of lines in the sand (al-khaṭṭ), and he ﷺ said: > **""It is a remnant of knowledge.""**"
"928","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said regarding the saying of Allah Mighty and Majestic: *{...or a remnant of knowledge}* [al-Aḥqāf: 4]: > **""It refers to the excellence of script and penmanship.""**"
"929","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Learn enough of your lineages to maintain your ties of kinship.""**"
"930","Al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Khārijah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Learn enough of your lineages to maintain your ties of kinship.""**"
"931","Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Ḥakam رضي الله عنه narrated: He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I wish to ask you about a matter concerning which I will never ask anyone after you: Who is our father?"" He ﷺ replied, **""Adam.""** He asked: ""Who is our mother?"" He ﷺ replied, **""Ḥawwāʾ (Eve).""** He asked: ""Who is the progenitor of the jinn?"" He ﷺ replied, **""Iblīs.""** He asked: ""Then who is their mother?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""His wife.""**"
"932","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The sons of Nūḥ (Noah) عليه السلام were Sām, Ḥām, and Yāfith. Sām fathered the Arabs, the Persians, and the Romans—and goodness is among them; Yāfith fathered Gog and Magog (Yaʾjūj and Maʾjūj), the Turks, and the Slavs—and there is no goodness in them; and Ḥām fathered the Copts, the Berbers, and the Sudanese.""**"
"933","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn and Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Nūḥ fathered three sons: Sām is the father of the Arabs, Ḥām is the father of the Abyssinians, and Yāfith is the father of the Romans.""**"
"934","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān ibn Udd ibn Udad ibn Zayd ibn Barāʾ ibn Aʿrāq al-Tharā.""** She said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite: > **""{...and [He destroyed] ʿĀd and Thamūd and the companions of the Rass and many generations between them} [al-Furqān: 38]—none knows them except Allah.""** Umm Salamah used to say: ""Maʿadd is Maʿadd, ʿAdnān is ʿAdnān, Udad is Udad, Zayd is Humaysaʿ, Barāʾ is Nabt, and Aʿrāq al-Tharā is Ismāʿīl son of Ibrāhīm عليهما السلام."""
"935","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said: > **""The lineage of the people is soundly and reliably established up to Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān.""**"
"936","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about Sabaʾ: ""What was he? A man, a woman, or a land?"" The Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""Rather, he was a man to whom ten sons were born: six of them settled in Yemen, and four settled in al-Shām (the Levant). As for the Yemenis, they are Madhḥij, Kindah, al-Azd, al-Ashʿariyyūn, Anmār, and Ḥimyar—all of them pure Arabs. As for the Levantines, they are Lakhm, Judhām, ʿĀmilah, and Ghassān.""**"
"937","ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: **""Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.""** So I stood up, but he ﷺ said, **""Sit down.""** He did that three times; each time I stood up and he said, **""Sit down.""** When it was the third time, I asked: ""From whom are we, O Messenger of Allah?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You—O gathering of Quḍāʿah—are from Ḥimyar.""** ʿAmr said: ""I kept this hadith concealed for twenty years."""
"938","ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه narrated: While we were in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, he said, **""Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.""** ʿAmr ibn Murrah stood up, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him, **""Sit down.""** He sat down. Then he ﷺ repeated, **""Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.""** ʿAmr stood up, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, **""Sit down.""** Then he ﷺ said a third time, **""Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.""** ʿAmr stood up, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, **""Sit down.""** He asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, from whom are we?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""You are from Quḍāʿah ibn Mālik ibn Ḥimyar—the well-known and undeniable lineage.""** ʿAmr said: ""I concealed this hadith until the days of Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, who sent for me and said: 'Would you ascend the pulpit and state that Quḍāʿah is the son of Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān, in exchange for which I will grant you the revenues of the two Iraqs (Kufa and Basra) and Egypt for as long as I live?' ʿAmr replied: 'Yes.' The call for congregational prayer was proclaimed, and the people assembled. ʿAmr came, stepping over the necks of the people, until he ascended the pulpit. He praised and glorified Allah, and then said: 'O people! Whoever knows me knows me, and whoever does not know me, I am ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī. Behold! Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān summoned me to mount the pulpit and claim that Quḍāʿah is the son of Maʿadd ibn ʿAdnān. Truly: *We are the sons of the noble, radiant elder,* *Quḍāʿah ibn Mālik ibn Ḥimyar,* *The well-known lineage, not to be denied!*' He then descended, and Muʿāwiyah said to him three times: 'Away with you, O treacherous one!' ʿAmr replied: 'It is as you saw, O Commander of the Faithful.' His son Zuhayr followed him and said: 'O my father, what harm would there have been for you had you obeyed the Commander of the Faithful and received the revenues of the two Iraqs and Egypt for his lifetime?' Thereupon ʿAmr recited: > **'Had I obeyed you, O Zuhayr, you would have clothed me among the people in broad daylight with a garment of shame! Qaḥṭān is our father to whom we trace our name, and the father of Khuzaymah is Khindif ibn Nizār. Is wandering in the darkness of night whose leaves fall excused among people, or wandering in the broad daylight? Shall we sell our father unto whom we are called in exchange for a father of strange clans, absent and hidden? That is a bargain the like of which we will never accept—pure gold being sold for lead and mere needles!'**"""
"939","Al-Rabīʿ ibn Sabrah reported from his father رضي الله عنه, who said: I was present with the Prophet ﷺ when he said, **""Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.""** ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī stood up, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him, **""Sit down.""** He did that three times, and then the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Quḍāʿah is from Ḥimyar.""**"
"940","Abū ʿUshshānah reported: I heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه say while upon the pulpit: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Medina while I was tending sheep belonging to me. I left them and went to him, saying: 'Will you accept my pledge of allegiance, O Messenger of Allah?' He ﷺ asked, **'From whom are you?'** I informed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, **'Which is more beloved to you: the pledge of migration (Hijrah) or the pledge of a desert-dweller (Bedouin)?'** I said, 'The pledge of migration,' and so he took my pledge. Then one day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, **'Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.'** I stood up, and he said, **'Sit down.'** Then he said, **'Whoever is here from Maʿadd, let him stand up.'** I stood up, and he said, **'Sit down.'** Then he said it a third time, so I stood up, and he said, **'Sit down.'** I asked: 'From whom are we, O Messenger of Allah?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You are from Quḍāʿah ibn Mālik ibn Ḥimyar.""**"
"941","Al-Jafshīsh al-Kindī رضي الله عنه narrated: A delegation from Kindah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""You are one of us,"" claiming him as their kin. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""We do not trace our ancestry through our mother, nor do we disown our father; we are the sons of al-Naḍr ibn Kinānah.""**"
"942","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""An ally of a people is one of them, the freed slave of a people is one of them, and the son of a people's sister is one of them.""**"
"943","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The son of a people's sister is one of them.""**"
"944","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Our freed slaves are from us.""**"
"945","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two freed slaves: an Abyssinian and a Copt. One day, they exchanged harsh words, and one of them said: ""O Abyssinian!"" while the other said: ""O Copt!"" Thereupon, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not say such things! You two are simply two men from the family of Muḥammad ﷺ.""**"
"946","ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān رضي الله عنه narrated: One day, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Quraysh: **""Is there anyone among you who does not belong to you?""** They replied: ""Our sister's son, ʿUtbah ibn Ghazwān."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The son of a people's sister is one of them [and the ally of a people is one of them].""**"
"947","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The son of a people's sister is one of them.""**"
"948","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The Islamic calendar commenced in the year the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Medina, and in that same year ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما was born.""**"
"949","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ was born on a Monday, commissioned as a Prophet on a Monday, departed migrating from Mecca to Medina on a Monday, entered Medina on a Monday, passed away on a Monday, and placed the Black Stone into position on a Monday.""** In another addition: > **""And he triumphed at Badr on a Monday, and Sūrat al-Māʾidah was revealed on a Monday: *{This day I have perfected for you your religion...}* [al-Māʾidah: 3].""**"
"950","Jarīr ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away at the age of sixty-three, Abū Bakr passed away at the age of sixty-three, and ʿUmar was martyred at the age of sixty-three.""**"
"951","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was sixty-five years of age.""**"
"952","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was born in the Year of the Elephant.""**"
"953","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه reported that a man asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, was Adam a prophet?"" He ﷺ replied: **""Yes.""** The man asked: ""How much time was there between him and Nūḥ?"" He ﷺ said: **""Ten centuries.""** The man asked: ""How much time was there between Nūḥ and Ibrāhīm?"" He ﷺ said: **""Ten centuries.""** The man asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, how many were the Messengers?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three hundred and fifteen.""**"
"954","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: He came to the Prophet ﷺ while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering a sermon, and sat down. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: **""Have you sought refuge from the evil of the devils among jinn and mankind?""** I asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, who was the first of the Prophets?"" He ﷺ replied: **""Adam.""** I asked: ""Was he a prophet?"" He ﷺ said: **""Yes, spoken to directly by Allah.""** I asked: ""Then who?"" He ﷺ said: **""Nūḥ, and between them were ten generations of fathers.""** I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, tell me about the prayer."" He ﷺ said: **""It is a prescribed good; whoever wills may do much of it.""** I asked: ""And charity?"" He ﷺ said: **""Multiplied manifold.""** I asked: ""And fasting?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Fasting is a shield. Allah has said: 'Fasting is for Me, and I shall reward for it.' By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, the smell of the mouth of a fasting person is more fragrant to Allah than the scent of musk.""** I asked: ""Which charity is most virtuous?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The striving of one of modest means, and charity given secretly to the poor.""** I asked: ""Which slave is most virtuous to emancipate?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The one highest in price and most valued by his owners.""**"
"955","Saʿīd ibn Yarbūʿ رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: **""Am I older (greater), or are you?""** He replied: > **""You are greater and far better than me, but I am older in years.""**"
"956","Daghfal ibn Ḥanẓalah رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was sixty-five years of age.""**"
"957","Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī reported: > **""The Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was sixty years of age.""**"
"958","Abū Ḥamzah reported from his father رضي الله عنه: > **""The Prophet ﷺ passed away when he was sixty-three years of age.""**"
"959","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The scriptures of Ibrāhīm were revealed on the first night of Ramaḍān; the Torah was revealed when six nights of Ramaḍān had passed; the Gospel was revealed when thirteen nights of Ramaḍān had passed; and the Qurʾān was revealed when twenty-four nights of Ramaḍān had passed.""**"
"960","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Allah revealed the scriptures of Ibrāhīm on the first night that passed of Ramaḍān; the Torah was revealed to Mūsā when six nights of Ramaḍān had passed; the Psalms (al-Zabūr) were revealed to Dāwūd on the eleventh night that passed of Ramaḍān; and the Qurʾān was revealed to Muḥammad ﷺ when twenty-four nights of Ramaḍān had passed.""**"
"961","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The companions of the Prophet ﷺ recounted to us that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""One hundred years will not arrive from the Hijrah with any blinking eye remaining alive from among you.""**"
"962","Nuʿaym ibn Dajājah reported: Abū Masʿūd ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr al-Anṣārī came to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنهما. ʿAlī said to him: ""Are you the one who says: 'A hundred years will not come with any blinking eye remaining upon the earth'? Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only said: > **'A hundred years will not pass over the people with any blinking eye remaining upon the earth from among those who are alive today.'** By Allah, the ease and prosperity of this Ummah will only come after a hundred years!"""
"963","Nuʿaym ibn Dajājah narrated: I was sitting with ʿAlī رضي الله عنه when Abū Masʿūd arrived. ʿAlī said, ""Farrukh has arrived,"" and he sat down. ʿAlī said, ""Do you issue verdicts to the people?"" He replied, ""Yes, and I inform them that the later times will be worse."" ʿAlī said, ""Then tell me, did you hear anything from him ﷺ?"" He said, ""Yes, I heard him say: **'A hundred years will not pass over the people with any blinking eye remaining upon the earth.'**"" ʿAlī said: ""You have completely missed the mark and erred in your very first verdict! He ﷺ only said that concerning those who were present with him on that day. For is prosperity to be expected except after the hundred years?"""
"964","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The most daring of people in questioning the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were the Bedouins. A Bedouin came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour?"" The Prophet ﷺ did not give him any answer until he entered the mosque and performed a brief prayer. Then the Prophet ﷺ turned to them and said, **""Where is the one who asked about the Hour?""** Just then, a young boy named Saʿd passed by him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If this boy lives to consume his natural lifespan, not a blinking eye among you will remain alive.""**"
"965","Sufyān ibn Wahb al-Khawlānī رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""A hundred years will not arrive with anyone remaining upon the surface of the earth.""**"
"966","Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī رضي الله عنه reported—sometimes raised to the Prophet ﷺ and sometimes stopped with the narrator: > **""Indeed, Allah Exalted will not fail to grant this Ummah a respite of half a day (five hundred years); and when you see the Levant become the banquet table of a single man and his household, then at that time Constantinople shall be conquered.""**"
"967","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Rāshid reported: I heard al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib رضي الله عنه, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when most people were saying: ""The final decree will be at one hundred""—meaning that the Day of Resurrection would occur at one hundred years. So al-Miqdām said: > **""You have exaggerated! Allah will never fail to grant this Ummah a delay of half a day—meaning five hundred years.""**"
"968","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""One hundred years will not expire with any blinking eye remaining alive.""**"
"969","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated in another narration: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, for Allah Blessed and Exalted there is a wind that He will send forth at the turn of a hundred years, and it will take the soul of every believer.""**"
"970","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: They were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the expedition of Tabūk, and he ﷺ said: > **""O people! Indeed, there is no soul alive today upon whom a hundred years will pass such that Allah will reckon anything of him (i.e., none of this generation will remain alive).""**"
"971","Abū al-Ṭufayl ʿĀmir ibn Wāthilah رضي الله عنه said: > **""I witnessed eight years of the life of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I was born in the year of the Battle of Uḥud.""**"
"972","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Prophet ﷺ was commissioned when I was a young boy carrying meat from the plain up to the mountain.""**"
"973","Abū al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنه said: > **""I witnessed eight years of the life of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I was born in the year of Uḥud.""** ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad said: My father said: ""Thābit came to us in Kufa and stayed in the city of Abū Jaʿfar, so I and Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn went to him and heard ahadith from him."""
"974","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Salʿ reported: I asked ʿAbd Khayr: ""How old are you?"" He replied: ""One hundred and twenty years."" I asked: ""Do you remember anything from the pre-Islamic days of ignorance?"" He replied: > **""Yes. We were in our lands in Yemen when the letter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us, inviting the people to vast goodness. My father was among those who went forth to meet him while I was a young boy. When my father returned, he said to my mother: 'Order this cooking pot to be spilled out for the dogs, for indeed we have embraced Islam!'""**"
"975","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, I believe that a man forgets knowledge that he once knew on account of a sin that he commits.""**"
"976","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه narrated: During the Farewell Pilgrimage, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up, with al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās riding behind him on a brownish camel, and said: > **""O people! Take of knowledge before knowledge is seized, and before it is lifted up!""** Allah Mighty and Majestic had revealed: *{O you who have believed, do not ask about things which, if they are shown to you, will distress you; but if you ask about them while the Qurʾān is being revealed, they will be shown to you. Allah has pardoned that, and Allah is Forgiving and Forbearing}* [al-Māʾidah: 101]. We had come to dislike questioning him excessively and were cautious after Allah revealed this to His Prophet ﷺ. So we went to a Bedouin and gave him a cloak as a gift, which he wrapped around his head as a turban—until I saw its fringe hanging over his right eyebrow. We said to him: ""Ask the Prophet ﷺ."" So he said: ""O Prophet of Allah, how will knowledge be lifted from us when the copies of the Book (al-Maṣāḥif) are among us, and we have learned what is in them and taught them to our wives, our children, and our servants?"" The Prophet ﷺ raised his head, and redness covered his face out of anger. Then he ﷺ said: > **""Woe to you! May your mother be bereaved of you! Here are the Jews and the Christians, having their scriptures among them, yet they do not adhere to a single letter of what their prophets brought them! Behold, indeed the departure of knowledge is the departure of its bearers!""** He ﷺ repeated this three times."
"977","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The time is near when knowledge will be lifted.""** He repeated it three times. Ziyād ibn Labīd said: ""O Prophet of Allah, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you! How will knowledge be lifted from us when this is the Book of Allah, which we recite and which our children recite to their children?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to him and said: > **""May your mother be bereaved of you, O Ziyād ibn Labīd! I used to count you among the scholars of Medina! Are not the Torah and the Gospel with these Jews, yet what did it avail them? Indeed, Allah does not take away knowledge by snatching it away; rather, He takes it away through the passing of its bearers. And no scholar departs from this Ummah except that a breach is made in Islam that cannot be sealed until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"978","ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked toward the sky and said: > **""This is the time when knowledge will be lifted.""** A man from the Anṣār named Ziyād ibn Labīd said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah, how, when it has been firmly established and hearts have preserved it?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""I used to consider you among the most understanding of the people of Medina!""** Then he ﷺ mentioned the misguidance of the Jews and Christians despite the Book of Allah in their hands. Jubayr ibn Nufayr said: I met Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه and related to him the hadith of ʿAwf, whereupon he said: ""ʿAwf spoke the truth. Shall I not inform you of the very first part of that to be lifted? It is humility (khushūʿ), until you will not see a single humble person."""
"979","Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The time is near when knowledge will be snatched away from the people until they will not be able to retain any part of it.""** Ziyād ibn Labīd said: ""How will knowledge be snatched from us when we have recited the Qurʾān and taught our children to recite it?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""May your mother be bereaved of you, O Ibn Labīd! Here are the Torah and the Gospel in the hands of the Jews and Christians, yet they pay no regard to them whatsoever!""**"
"980","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah does not seize knowledge by wresting it away from the people; rather, He seizes knowledge by taking away the scholars. When the scholars are gone, the people will take ignorant leaders as heads. They will be questioned and will issue verdicts without knowledge, thus straying and leading others astray from the right path.""**"
"981","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah does not wrest knowledge away from you after having bestowed it upon you; rather, He takes the scholars along with their knowledge, and ignorant people will remain. They will be questioned, and they will issue verdicts, thereby leading others astray and going astray themselves.""**"
"982","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah will seize the scholars and seize knowledge along with them, whereupon immature youths will emerge, contending recklessly with one another, and among them the elder will be deemed weak.""**"
"983","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah Blessed and Exalted does not wrest knowledge away from the people after having granted it to them; rather, He takes away the scholars. Whenever a scholar departs, he departs with what he possesses of knowledge, until none remain except those who do not know, and so they go astray and lead others astray.""**"
"984","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported as a prophetic saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The death of a scholar is a breach in Islam that cannot be sealed so long as night and day alternate.""**"
"985","Ṣafwān ibn ʿAssāl رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged the pursuit of knowledge before its departure. A man asked: ""How will it depart when we have learned it and taught it to our children?"" The Prophet ﷺ became angry and said: > **""Are not the Torah and the Gospel in the hands of the People of the Scripture? Did it avail them anything?""**"
"986","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The death of a scholar is a calamity that cannot be mended and a breach that cannot be sealed; he is a star that has been extinguished. Indeed, the death of an entire tribe is easier for me to bear than the death of a single scholar.""**"
"987","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the likeness of the scholars is like that of the stars in the heaven by which guidance is sought in the darkness of the land and the sea; when the stars are extinguished, those who seek guidance are soon to go astray.""**"
"988","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Tribulations will increase, turmoil and bloodshed will proliferate, and knowledge will be lifted.""** When ʿUmar heard Abū Hurayrah saying ""knowledge will be lifted,"" ʿUmar رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, it is not wrested from the chests of men, but rather the scholars depart.""**"
"989","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""This Ummah will continue to be upright upon an established path (Sharīʿah) so long as three things do not appear among them: so long as knowledge is not seized from them, illegitimate offspring do not multiply among them, and the Saqqārūn do not appear among them.""** It was said: ""And who are the Saqqārūn, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: > **""People who will be at the end of time, whose greeting among themselves is mutual cursing.""**"
"990","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه asked: ""Do you know how Islam diminishes?"" They replied: ""Just as the dye of a garment fades, and just as the fatness of a riding beast dwindles, and just as a silver dirham wears away from long keeping in a purse."" Ibn Masʿūd said: > **""Indeed, that is part of it, but greater than all of that is the death and departure of the scholars.""**"
"991","Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab said: I attended the funeral of Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه. When he was buried in his grave, Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""O people! Whoever is pleased to know how knowledge departs, this is how knowledge departs! By Allah, an abundance of knowledge has departed this day!""**"
"992","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Do you know what the departure of knowledge is? It is the departure of the scholars from the earth.""**"
